\": \"---\\nname: fleet-dispatch-verification\\ndescription: \\\"Use when dispatching to a specialist; verify the result.\\\"\\nversion: 1.0.0\\nauthor: Hermes Agent\\nlicense: MIT\\nplatforms: [linux, macos, windows]\\nmetadata:\\n  hermes:\\n    tags: [multi-agent, orchestration, delegation, verification, fleet, dispatch]\\n    related_skills: [agent-fleet-orchestration, hermes-agent]\\n---\\n\\n# Fleet Dispatch & Verification\\n\\nThe manager's per-task loop when specialists are Hermes profiles invoked by shell.\\n\\n**Scope:** standing the fleet up (creating profiles, writing SOUL role cards, choosing\\nbetween dispatch / Bot Mode / Kanban) is the bundled `agent-fleet-orchestration` skill.\\nThis skill covers the other half: routing a real task to a specialist and deciding\\nwhether what came back is true.\\n\\n## When to Use\\n\\n- About to hand work to a specialist profile: `hermes -p <agent> chat -q \\\"<brief>\\\"`.\\n- A specialist returned a report and you must decide whether to trust it.\\n- A routing table or `profile.yaml` names an agent role you need to act on.\\n\\nFan-out is for work that genuinely splits. A question you already know the answer to,\\na single file read, or one command you can run yourself gets a direct answer — dispatch\\nno specialist to confirm your own conclusion.\\n\\n## Procedure\\n\\n### 1. Audit the roster before you route\\n\\n**Routing tables drift, and dispatch fails hard when they do.** A routing table (a SOUL\\nbody, a `profile.yaml` description, or a manager's own instructions) can name an agent\\nthat was deleted. The command then prints\\n`Error: Profile '<name>' does not exist.` and no work happens — while a careful-looking\\nreport is what you think you will get. Check before you rely on any route:\\n\\n```bash\\nhermes profile list                      # authoritative roster; active profile marked ◆\\nls ~/.hermes/profiles/                   # live profile dirs\\nls ~/.hermes/profiles/.deleted/          # retired names — present here means GONE\\nls ~/.local/bin/ | grep -Ev '^total'     # which aliases still exist\\n```\\n\\n- A name in `.deleted/` is retired even if a routing table still lists it.\\n- **`profile.yaml`'s `description` drifts the same way.** When you add or remove a\\n  specialist, update the manager's routing table *and* its `profile.yaml` description in\\n  the same change — a stale description is read by the kanban decomposer for routing.\\n- Confirm no stale alias survives: `~/.local/bin/<retired-name>` should be gone, or the\\n  old agent stays invocable by name.\\n- Fix a broken route before dispatching (recreate the profile, or drop the row and say\\n  which you did). Do not dispatch into a route you know is dead.\\n\\n### 2. Write the brief to a file, then pass it by command substitution\\n\\nA multi-line brief containing quotes, apostrophes, `$(...)` or backticks gets mangled or\\nrejected inline. Write it out, then dispatch from the file:\\n\\n```bash\\n# write_file the brief to a scratch path first, then:\\nhermes -p <agent> chat -q \\\"$(cat /abs/path/brief.txt)\\\"\\n```\\n\\nThis also makes the brief reviewable, diffable, and re-runnable when you need to send\\ncorrected work in round two.\\n\\nSee `templates/dispatch-brief.md` for the section skeleton that works.\\n\\n### 3. Every brief needs five things\\n\\nGoal · exact deliverable paths · required features · constraints · **an evidence demand**.\\n\\n- **Absolute paths, never \\\"the file we discussed\\\"** — the specialist cannot see your\\n  conversation.\\n- Name the deliverable directory and the exact files expected, plus what must *not* be\\n  created (no build pipeline, no lockfiles, do not touch path X).\\n- **Demand handles, not intent.** Specify the commands you want run and their real output:\\n  counts, `ls -la`, HTTP status lines, `path:line`, console output, screenshot absolute\\n  paths. \\\"It works\\\" and \\\"done\\\" are not evidence.\\n- Tell it plainly that a reported failure beats a fabricated success. Specialists optimise\\n  for looking finished; say the opposite out loud.\\n- Match scope to the ask. Over-specifying a small job wastes a round; under-specifying\\n  guarantees one.\\n\\n### 4. Verify independently — on your own resources\\n\\nA specialist's self-report is not proof. Reproduce the claims yourself:\\n\\n- **Start your own server on a DIFFERENT port.** If the specialist killed its server as\\n  asked, there is nothing of its left to check; if it left one running, you would be\\n  testing its process rather than a fresh load of the artifact. A separate port gives you\\n  a clean, reproducible surface.\\n- **Re-derive every number yourself.** `wc -c` the files against the reported sizes, count\\n  the rendered elements, re-read the exit codes. Mismatched sizes are a cheap catch.\\n- **Target the claim a self-report is most likely to have got wrong:** the bug it says it\\n  fixed. Read the fix in the source, then drive the artifact into the failing state and\\n  confirm it actually holds. A specialist reporting a fix is exactly where a plausible\\n  story substitutes for a working one.\\n- **Check the artifact's own gates:** syntax checks (`node --check`, the language\\n  equivalent), browser console messages and JS errors, HTTP status lines.\\n- **Re-open every screenshot before repeating its label.** A mislabelled evidence file is\\n  worse than no evidence; open the image and confirm what it actually shows.\\n- **Exercise the interaction, do not just read the DOM.** Type into the real input, click\\n  the real control, and read the resulting state back.\\n- Only after this do you deliver a claim. Report what *you* observed, and separate it from\\n  what the specialist asserted.\\n\\n### 5. Kill verification servers by PID\\n\\nRecord the PID when you launch (`background=true` returns it) and use `kill <pid>`.\\n**`pkill -f \\\"<pattern>\\\"` matches the shell running that very command line and SIGTERMs\\nyour own session** — the command dies mid-pipeline and you lose the output it was about to\\nprint. Verify the port is actually closed afterwards:\\n\\n```bash\\nprintf 'port check: '; curl -s -m 3 -o /dev/null -w \\\"%{http_code}\\\\n\\\" http://localhost:<port>/\\n# 000 = down\\nps -eo pid,args | grep -F 'http.server' | grep -vF grep || echo \\\"none\\\"\\n```\\n\\n### 6. Deliver one integrated answer\\n\\nResolved goal in one line · the result stated first · who did what (which agent produced\\nwhich part, what you verified) · evidence as handles, not adjectives · what nobody checked\\nand what it would take to close it. Carry the specialist's caveats through intact — do not\\nsmooth over a limitation it reported.\\n\\n## Pitfalls\\n\\n- **Verify the roster, not the intent.** A routing table is a plan; `hermes profile list`\\n  is the truth. Routing to a deleted agent produces an error message and no work.\\n- **Deleting a specialist is a multi-file change.** The profile, its alias, the manager's\\n  routing table, and the manager's `profile.yaml` description all move together.\\n- **The one claim you must not take on trust is the fixed bug.** Everything else in a\\n  report is usually accurate; the fix is where optimism lives.\\n- **Never paste a specialist's transcript as your answer.** Carry its findings, caveats and\\n  handles; keep your own verification visibly separate from its assertions.\\n- **Do not reuse the specialist's port or its server.** Independently loaded beats\\n  inherited.\\n- **A specialist that reports an environment as incapable may not have tried.** Assert its\\n  environment claims yourself before accepting a \\\"could not run it\\\" as a blocker.\\n- **Skill descriptions are capped at 60 chars** (trigger first, ending in a period) and a\\n  longer one aborts the create outright.\\n- **Cap the loop.** Two rounds per specialist. If it is still failing, report the blocker\\n  with the exact command and error rather than dispatching a third corrected brief.\\n\", \"path\": \"fleet-dispatch-verification/SKILL.md\", \"skill_dir\": \"/home/sudhanshunaik/.hermes/profiles/manager/skills/fleet-dispatch-verification\", \"org_provenance\": null, \"linked_files\": {\"templates\": [\"templates/dispatch-brief.md\"]}, \"usage_hint\": \"To view linked files, call skill_view(name, file_path) where file_path is e.g. 'references/api.md' or 'assets/config.yaml'\", \"required_environment_variables\": [], \"required_commands\": [], \"missing_required_environment_variables\": [], \"missing_credential_files\": [], \"missing_required_commands\": [], \"setup_needed\": false, \"setup_skipped\": false, \"readiness_status\": \"available\", \"_source_path\": \"/home/sudhanshunaik/.hermes/profiles/manager/skills/fleet-dispatch-verification/SKILL.md\", \"metadata\": {\"hermes\": {\"tags\": [\"multi-agent\", \"orchestration\", \"delegation\", \"verification\", \"fleet\", \"dispatch\"], \"related_skills\": [\"agent-fleet-orchestration\", \"hermes-agent\"]}}}", "tool_call_id": "call_00_Hr0WBJycqLjMIOHSApXb7058", "tool_calls": null, "tool_name": "skill_view", "effect_disposition": null, "timestamp": 1790789093.767772, "token_count": null, "finish_reason": null, "reasoning": null, "reasoning_content": null, "reasoning_details": null, "codex_reasoning_items": null, "codex_message_items": null, "platform_message_id": null, "observed": 0, "active": 1, "compacted": 0, "api_content": null, "display_kind": null, "display_metadata": null, "message_uid": "e46d34b0791445f29843c35957b1730a", "absorbed_message_uids": null, "tool_call_uids": null, "tool_call_uid": "61ce54b8709b44efbbde02a1905f4eee"}, {"id": 208, "session_id": "20260930_225432_76ed87", "role": "tool", "content": "{\"success\": true, \"name\": \"agent-fleet-orchestration\", \"description\": \"Build a manager agent that dispatches to specialist agents.\", \"tags\": [\"multi-agent\", \"orchestration\", \"profiles\", \"bot-mode\", \"kanban\", \"delegation\", \"fleet\"], \"related_skills\": [\"hermes-agent\"], \"content\": \"---\\nname: agent-fleet-orchestration\\ndescription: \\\"Build a manager agent that dispatches to specialist agents.\\\"\\nversion: 1.0.0\\nauthor: Hermes Agent\\nlicense: MIT\\nplatforms: [linux, macos, windows]\\nmetadata:\\n  hermes:\\n    tags: [multi-agent, orchestration, profiles, bot-mode, kanban, delegation, fleet]\\n    related_skills: [hermes-agent]\\n---\\n\\n# Agent Fleet Orchestration\\n\\nBuild a **manager agent** that decomposes work and dispatches to **specialist agents**, all as Hermes profiles.\\n\\n## When to Use\\n\\n- The user wants a \\\"manager agent\\\", \\\"orchestrator\\\", \\\"team lead\\\", or \\\"agent that manages other agents\\\".\\n- The user wants named specialists (researcher, coder, analyst, writer) that keep their own persona, memory, and skills.\\n- An existing multi-agent setup needs a routing protocol, an evidence contract, or a dispatch loop that actually works headless.\\n\\nDo **not** use this for one-off parallel subtasks inside a single conversation — that is plain `delegate_task`.\\n\\n## The primitive: a specialist is a profile\\n\\nThere is no separate \\\"subagent\\\" object to invent. A specialist agent **is** a Hermes profile — its own `config.yaml`, `.env`, `SOUL.md`, skills, memory, and sessions under `~/.hermes/profiles/<name>/`.\\n\\nThat gives three coordinated layers, all over one primitive:\\n\\n| Layer | What it is | Use when |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| **Profiles + shell dispatch** | `hermes -p <agent> chat -q \\\"<brief>\\\"` | Manager needs a real specialist persona, memory, and skills. Works headless. **Default choice.** |\\n| **Bot Mode** (desktop) | Named Bots roster, group chats, bot-to-bot DMs | Humans watching agents collaborate. Desktop-only UI. |\\n| **Kanban** | Durable SQLite task board, dispatcher routes by `profile.yaml` description | Long-running multi-profile work that must survive restarts. |\\n\\n## Build a fleet\\n\\n### 1. Create profiles with working credentials\\n\\n```bash\\nhermes profile create <agent> --clone --description \\\"<role the router can act on>\\\"\\n```\\n\\n- `--clone` copies `config.yaml`, `.env` (API keys), `SOUL.md`, skills, and memories — **without it the specialist has no credentials and cannot run.**\\n- `--description` is read by the **kanban decomposer** to route by role instead of by name. Write it as a capability statement, not a job title.\\n- Sets up a shell alias at `~/.local/bin/<agent>`, so `chief` alone launches that agent.\\n\\n### 2. Overwrite each SOUL.md with a role card\\n\\n`--clone` copies the parent's SOUL verbatim; replace it per agent. A role card needs:\\n\\n- **Lane** — what it owns, stated concretely\\n- **Anti-lane** — what it must hand back rather than half-do (`\\\"if it's really a build task, say so and hand it back\\\"`)\\n- **Output contract** — the exact shape of a good return, including a demand for evidence\\n- **Refusal rule** — the failure mode you most want prevented\\n\\nThe **manager's** card additionally carries the routing table and the dispatch protocol below.\\n\\n### 3. The dispatch protocol (manager's SOUL)\\n\\n```bash\\nhermes -p <agent> chat -q \\\"<complete, self-contained brief>\\\"\\n```\\n\\nCritical: **the specialist cannot see the manager's conversation.** A brief must stand alone — goal, deliverable format, absolute paths for inputs, constraints, and a demand for evidence (command output, source URL, computed numbers) rather than statements of intent.\\n\\nFan-out loop: **decompose → dispatch (independent briefs in parallel) → aggregate → verify → deliver one integrated answer.**\\n\\n### 4. Verify end to end — do not skip\\n\\n```bash\\nhermes -p <specialist> chat -q \\\"In two lines: your name, your lane, one thing you refuse.\\\"\\n```\\n\\nProves the persona loaded. Then prove the **dispatch loop** with a real task requiring a specialist, and confirm the manager returned an integrated answer with evidence — not a plausible summary.\\n\\n## Pitfalls\\n\\n- **No `--clone` = broken agent.** The profile will look created but fail on first run with no credentials.\\n- **Cloning copies the parent SOUL.** If you forget to overwrite it, every \\\"specialist\\\" answers as the generic parent agent — the fleet is cosmetic.\\n- **Ask for evidence, not intent.** A specialist self-report (`\\\"done\\\"`, `\\\"uploaded\\\"`) is not proof. For external side effects, require a handle — URL, commit SHA, absolute path — and check it from the manager.\\n- **Over-delegation is a failure mode.** Encode explicitly in the manager SOUL that a trivial ask gets a direct answer; fan-out is only for work that genuinely splits.\\n- **`hermes profile describe --all` requires `--auto`.** To read a single description, pass the name.\\n- **Bot Mode is desktop-only.** Profiles built from the CLI appear in the Bots tab automatically; group chats and bot-to-bot DMs have no CLI equivalent.\\n- **Local model trees are shared** when cloning from the default profile — `models/`, `runtimes/`, `node/` are not duplicated per profile.\\n- **Skill descriptions are capped at 60 chars** in the system-prompt index. A longer one aborts the create; keep it to one short sentence and put detail in the body.\\n- **A specialist will report an environment as \\\"incapable\\\" without trying to install anything.** Patching its SOUL with the concrete fallback fixes it permanently. For Python: `uv run --with <pkg> python -m pytest` installs a missing tool for one command with no project changes. Validate the fleet by asserting its environment claims yourself — a plausible-sounding \\\"the sandbox would not permit it\\\" is exactly the kind of unverified self-report a manager must check.\\n- **`hermes profile delete` can leave pending identity.** If it warns that session/routing identity settlement is pending, run `hermes profile purge-identity <name>` or the name stays half-reserved.\\n- **Deleting a profile does not always remove its shell alias.** Verify `~/.local/bin/<old-name>` is gone after a roster change, or the old agent stays invocable by name.\\n\\n## Adding a specialist later\\n\\nSame two steps: `hermes profile create <name> --clone --description \\\"...\\\"`, then write its `SOUL.md` and add a row to the manager's routing table. No restart, no registry.\\n\", \"path\": \"autonomous-ai-agents/agent-fleet-orchestration/SKILL.md\", \"skill_dir\": \"/home/sudhanshunaik/.hermes/profiles/manager/skills/autonomous-ai-agents/agent-fleet-orchestration\", \"org_provenance\": null, \"linked_files\": null, \"usage_hint\": null, \"required_environment_variables\": [], \"required_commands\": [], \"missing_required_environment_variables\": [], \"missing_credential_files\": [], \"missing_required_commands\": [], \"setup_needed\": false, \"setup_skipped\": false, \"readiness_status\": \"available\", \"_source_path\": \"/home/sudhanshunaik/.hermes/profiles/manager/skills/autonomous-ai-agents/agent-fleet-orchestration/SKILL.md\", \"metadata\": {\"hermes\": {\"tags\": [\"multi-agent\", \"orchestration\", \"profiles\", \"bot-mode\", \"kanban\", \"delegation\", \"fleet\"], \"related_skills\": [\"hermes-agent\"]}}}", "tool_call_id": "call_01_Xv55j9AnOkkTq5md8M6P0510", "tool_calls": null, "tool_name": "skill_view", "effect_disposition": null, "timestamp": 1790789093.7754261, "token_count": null, "finish_reason": null, "reasoning": null, "reasoning_content": null, "reasoning_details": null, "codex_reasoning_items": null, "codex_message_items": null, "platform_message_id": null, "observed": 0, "active": 1, "compacted": 0, "api_content": null, "display_kind": null, "display_metadata": null, "message_uid": "43def7b39fa3478aa8dca84344df589a", "absorbed_message_uids": null, "tool_call_uids": null, "tool_call_uid": "f59019e39fc3418ab443a29a99897163"}, {"id": 209, "session_id": "20260930_225432_76ed87", "role": "tool", "content": "{\"output\": \"/home/sudhanshunaik/Projects/Projects/Rescape Hermes/hermes-agent/.hermes/bin/hermes\\nHermes Agent v