567e9-9ff8-44be-8e52-52b56b3a7496", "tool_calls": null, "tool_name": "read_file", "effect_disposition": null, "timestamp": 1790791170.150224, "token_count": null, "finish_reason": null, "reasoning": null, "reasoning_content": null, "reasoning_details": null, "codex_reasoning_items": null, "codex_message_items": null, "platform_message_id": null, "observed": 0, "active": 1, "compacted": 0, "api_content": null, "display_kind": null, "display_metadata": null, "message_uid": "884481dc5ae44f1aaf2ac38dca81e15b", "absorbed_message_uids": null, "tool_call_uids": null, "tool_call_uid": "7344089b0d384446a9188026dfd33e22"}, {"id": 243, "session_id": "20260930_225432_76ed87", "role": "tool", "content": "{\"content\": \"1|# GPU specification dataset — verified facts, sources and gaps\\n2|\\n3|Companion to `gpus.json` (14 records). Every number in the JSON traces to a URL listed in this file\\n4|or in that record's own `sources` array. Values that could not be verified are `null` in the JSON and\\n5|are itemised under \\\"Numbers I could NOT verify\\\". Nothing here is interpolated from a different\\n6|figure — in particular, no bandwidth value is derived from bus width or memory type.\\n7|\\n8|---\\n9|\\n10|## How this was built, and exactly what was reachable\\n11|\\n12|### URLs fetched directly (real read, not search snippets)\\n13|\\n14|**NVIDIA — official product/datasheet pages**\\n15|\\n16|- https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/geforce/graphics-cards/compare/ — NVIDIA's own 50-series and 40-series spec tables\\n17|- https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/geforce/graphics-cards/50-series/rtx-5090/\\n18|- https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/data-center/h100/\\n19|- https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/data-center/h200/\\n20|- https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/data-center/a100/\\n21|- https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/data-center/b200/ (serves the HGX platform page)\\n22|- https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/data-center/technologies/blackwell-architecture\\n23|- https://www.nvidia.com/content/dam/en-zz/Solutions/Data-Center/a100/pdf/nvidia-a100-datasheet-us-nvidia-1758950-r4-web.pdf (PDF, A100)\\n24|- https://www.pny.com/File%20Library/Company/Support/Product%20Brochures/NVIDIA%20Data%20Center%20GPUs/english/nvidia-h100-datasheet.pdf (PDF — NVIDIA's own H100 datasheet, hosted by PNY)\\n25|- https://dam-cdn.nvd.orangelogic.com/AssetLink/y441155802qub41q118b2852i557jem5.pdf (PDF — NVIDIA Blackwell datasheet)\\n26|\\n27|**AMD — official product pages and datasheets**\\n28|\\n29|- https://www.amd.com/en/products/graphics/desktops/radeon/7000-series/amd-radeon-rx-7900xtx.html\\n30|- https://www.amd.com/en/products/graphics/desktops/radeon/9000-series/amd-radeon-rx-9070xt.html\\n31|- https://www.amd.com/content/dam/amd/en/documents/instinct-tech-docs/data-sheets/amd-instinct-mi300x-data-sheet.pdf (PDF)\\n32|- https://www.amd.com/en/newsroom/press-releases/2022-11-3-amd-unveils-world-s-most-advanced-gaming-graphics-.html (launch SEP)\\n33|\\n34|**Intel — official product specification pages**\\n35|\\n36|- https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/products/sku/241598/intel-arc-b580-graphics/specifications.html\\n37|- https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/products/sku/229151/intel-arc-a770-graphics-16gb/specifications.html\\n38|\\n39|**TechPowerUp GPU database (fetched while the site was still serving this session)**\\n40|\\n41|- https://www.techpowerup.com/gpu-specs/geforce-rtx-5090.c4216\\n42|- https://www.techpowerup.com/gpu-specs/geforce-rtx-5080.c4217\\n43|- https://www.techpowerup.com/gpu-specs/geforce-rtx-5070.c4218\\n44|- https://www.techpowerup.com/gpu-specs/geforce-rtx-4090.c3889\\n45|- https://www.techpowerup.com/gpu-specs/geforce-rtx-4080-super.c4182\\n46|\\n47|**Other**\\n48|\\n49|- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CUDA\\n50|- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hopper_(microarchitecture)\\n51|\\n52|### Blocked or failed — routes tried, and what I did instead\\n53|\\n54|- **TechPowerUp GPU database went behind an anti-bot wall mid-run.** The five GeForce pages above were\\n55|  fetched successfully; after that, `techpowerup.com/gpu-specs/…` began returning `403 - Access Denied`\\n56|  and an \\\"Automated bot check in progress / drag the handle to the target\\\" interstitial. Three distinct\\n57|  routes were tried: (1) the extraction tool, (2) `curl` with a desktop User-Agent (which returns only a\\n58|  ~118 KB HTML shell with no spec table, because the specs are injected client-side), and (3) a real\\n59|  headless browser (which got the bot-check page, ~206 characters of text). That is why\\n60|  `h100-sxm5-80-gb`, `h200-sxm-141-gb`, `a100-sxm4-80-gb`, `radeon-rx-7900-xtx`, `radeon-rx-9070-xt`\\n61|  and `arc-a770` are **not** in the \\\"fetched directly\\\" list even though their numbers appear in this\\n62|  document. Where their figures were used, it is stated per-value below.\\n63|- **NVIDIA's `resources.nvidia.com` datasheet portal returns an HTML shell, not the PDF**, on direct\\n64|  request (I fetched `resources.nvidia.com/en-us-data-center-overview/nvidia-tensor-core-gpu-datasheet`\\n65|  and `…/en-us-dgx-systems/dgx-b200-datasheet` and both came back as HTML). Workaround: the same H100\\n66|  datasheet PDF and the Blackwell datasheet PDF were fetched from mirrors that serve the identical\\n67|  NVIDIA document (PNY-hosted H100 datasheet; `dam-cdn.nvd.orangelogic.com`-hosted Blackwell datasheet).\\n68|- **NVIDIA's Ampere architecture whitepaper could not be fetched.** The mirror at\\n69|  `https://www.techpowerup.com/gpu-specs/docs/nvidia-ampere-architecture.pdf` returned the bot wall's\\n70|  HTML instead of the PDF. Its two relevant sentences were therefore read through a search-engine\\n71|  rendering of that PDF: *\\\"64 FP32 CUDA Cores/SM, 6912 FP32 CUDA Cores per GPU… 4 Third-generation\\n72|  Tensor Cores/SM, 432 Third-generation Tensor Cores per GPU.\\\"* Flagged per-value below.\\n73|- **Intel ARK blocks `curl`** (403) but served fine through the extraction tool; both Intel pages above\\n74|  are genuine first-hand reads.\\n75|- Two of the search backends were intermittently unavailable during the run (`403 Forbidden` from a\\n76|  keyless search API), which is why some lookups were retried rather than answered first time.\\n77|\\n78|### Confidence rule used in `gpus.json`\\n79|\\n80|- `high` — every key field traces to a vendor-published figure (or to an official vendor datasheet).\\n81|- `medium` — at least one key field exists only in a third-party database (TechPowerUp) or a\\n82|  search-index rendering rather than a vendor page.\\n83|\\n84|### Note on the key set\\n85|\\n86|The brief listed 20 keys and then required the AI-throughput unit to be stated \\\"in `aiTopsUnit`\\\".\\n87|`aiTopsUnit` is therefore present as a 21st key on every record — without it `aiTops` has no defined\\n88|unit. All 14 records carry the identical 21-key set. The `aiTops` basis is **dense** throughout, but\\n89|it is *not* the same precision for every row: the NVIDIA H100/H200/B200/A100 rows and the AMD/Intel\\n90|consumer rows are dense **INT8**, while Instinct MI300X is dense **FP8** (AMD's data sheet publishes\\n91|no dense INT8 figure for MI300X). Each row's `aiTopsUnit` states which.\\n92|\\n93|---\\n94|\\n95|## Spec table\\n96|\\n97|| GPU | Vendor | Segment | Architecture | Year | Node | VRAM GB | Type | Bus bits | BW GB/s | TDP W | MSRP USD | FP32 TFLOPS | AI TOPS | Shader cores | Tensor cores | RT cores |\\n98||---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n99|| GeForce RTX 5090 | NVIDIA | consumer-desktop | Blackwell (GB202) | 2025 | TSMC 4N | 32 | GDDR7 | 512 | 1792 | 575 | 1999 | 104.8 | 838 | 21760 | 680 | 170 |\\n100|| GeForce RTX 5080 | NVIDIA | consumer-desktop | Blackwell (GB203) | 2025 | TSMC 4N | 16 | GDDR7 | 256 | 960 | 360 | 999 | 56.28 | 450.2 | 10752 | 336 | 84 |\\n101|| GeForce RTX 5070 | NVIDIA | consumer-desktop | Blackwell (GB205) | 2025 | TSMC 4N | 12 | GDDR7 | 192 | 672 | 250 | 549 | 30.87 | 246.9 | 6144 | 192 | 48 |\\n102|| GeForce RTX 4090 | NVIDIA | consumer-desktop | Ada Lovelace (AD102) | 2022 | TSMC 4N | 24 | GDDR6X | 384 | 1008 | 450 | 1599 | 83.0 | 660.6 | 16384 | 512 | 128 |\\n103|| GeForce RTX 4080 SUPER | NVIDIA | consumer-desktop | Ada Lovelace (AD103) | 2024 | TSMC 4N | 16 | GDDR6X | 256 | 736.3 | 320 | 999 | 52.0 | null | 10240 | 320 | 80 |\\n104|| H100 SXM5 80GB | NVIDIA | datacenter | Hopper (GH100) | 2022 | TSMC 4N | 80 | HBM3 | 5120 | 3350 | 700 | null | 67.0 | 1979 | 16896 | 528 | 0 |\\n105|| H200 SXM 141GB | NVIDIA | datacenter | Hopper (GH100) | 2023 | TSMC 4N | 141 | HBM3e | 6144 | 4800 | 700 | null | 67.0 | 1979 | 16896 | 528 | 0 |\\n106|| B200 (Blackwell SXM6) | NVIDIA | datacenter | Blackwell (dual-die) | 2024 | TSMC 4NP | 180 | HBM3e | null | 7700 | 1000 | null | 75.0 | 4500 | null | null | null |\\n107|| A100 80GB SXM4 | NVIDIA | datacenter | Ampere (GA100) | 2020 | TSMC 7 nm | 80 | HBM2e | 5120 | 2039 | 400 | null | 19.5 | 624 | 6912 | 432 | 0 |\\n108|| Radeon RX 9070 XT | AMD | consumer-desktop | RDNA 4 (Navi 48) | 2025 | TSMC N4P | 16 | GDDR6 | 256 | 640 | 304 | 599 | 48.7 | 389 | 4096 | 128 | 64 |\\n109|| Radeon RX 7900 XTX | AMD | consumer-desktop | RDNA 3 (Navi 31, chiplet) | 2022 | 5 nm + 6 nm | 24 | GDDR6 | 384 | 960 | 355 | 999 | 61.4 | 123 | 6144 | 192 | 96 |\\n110|| Instinct MI300X | AMD | datacenter | CDNA 3 (8 XCD + 4 IOD) | 2023 | 5 nm + 6 nm | 192 | HBM3 | 8192 | 5300 | 750 | null | 163.4 | 2614.9 | 19456 | 1216 | null |\\n111|| Arc B580 | Intel | consumer-desktop | Xe2 (Battlemage) | 2024 | TSMC N5 | 12 | GDDR6 | 192 | 456 | 190 | 249 | null | 233 | null | 160 | 20 |\\n112|| Arc A770 (16GB) | Intel | consumer-desktop | Xe HPG (Alchemist) | 2022 | TSMC N6 | 16 | GDDR6 | 256 | 560 | 225 | null | null | 262 | null | 512 | 32 |\\n113|\\n114|Column notes:\\n115|\\n116|- **AI TOPS** is the *dense* matrix figure in every row; see the basis note above. `null` for the\\n117|  RTX 4080 SUPER means a dense figure could not be verified, not that the card has no AI throughput.\\n118|- **Tensor cores** is each vendor's own name for the unit: NVIDIA tensor cores, AMD \\\"AI accelerators\\\"\\n119|  (consumer) / \\\"matrix cores\\\" (Instinct), Intel XMX engines. There is no component AMD calls a tensor\\n120|  core — the mapping is stated in each record's `notes`.\\n121|- **RT cores** is NVIDIA RT cores, AMD ray accelerators, Intel ray-tracing units. **`0` means the part\\n122|  has no ray-tracing hardware at all** (H100, H200, A100 — Hopper and Ampere datacenter silicon is\\n123|  compute-only). `null` for MI300X and B200 means *could not verify* rather than \\\"zero\\\".\\n124|- **Shader cores** is `null` for all three Intel rows and for B200 because Intel publishes Xe-core /\\n125|  XMX-engine / Xe-Vector-Engine counts and no \\\"shader cores\\\" count, and NVIDIA publishes no CUDA-core\\n126|  count for B200. The values were left null rather than silently equating unlike units.\\n127|- **`null` MSRP** means no vendor list price exists for that part (all datacenter accelerators are\\n128|  priced by quote), not that the price is unknown-but-guessable.\\n129|\\n130|---\\n131|\\n132|## Key facts\\n133|\\n134|Each claim below is a statement a website might make, followed by the source URL.\\n135|\\n136|1. **The RTX 5090 pairs 21,760 CUDA cores with 32 GB of GDDR7 on a 512-bit bus for 1,792 GB/s** —\\n137|   NVIDIA's own comparison table lists the bandwidth explicitly, so no derivation is involved\\n138|   (https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/geforce/graphics-cards/compare/).\\n139|\\n140|2. **The RTX 5090's 575 W TGP is the highest of any GeForce card, and NVIDIA recommends a 1,000 W\\n141|   system power supply** for it (https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/geforce/graphics-cards/50-series/rtx-5090/).\\n142|\\n143|3. **RTX 50-series launch prices were $1,999 (5090), $999 (5080) and $549 (5070); RTX 40-series were\\n144|   $1,599 (4090) and $999 (4080 SUPER).** The 5090's price is stated on NVIDIA's product page; the\\n145|   others are the launch prices recorded in the TechPowerUp GPU database entries for those exact SKUs\\n146|   (https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/geforce/graphics-cards/50-series/rtx-5090/,\\n147|   https://www.techpowerup.com/gpu-specs/geforce-rtx-5090.c4216,\\n148|   https://www.techpowerup.com/gpu-specs/geforce-rtx-4080-super.c4182).\\n149|\\n150|4. **H100 SXM5 delivers 80 GB of HBM3 at 3.35 TB/s with an \\\"Up to 700W (configurable)\\\" TDP and\\n151|   900 GB/s NVLink** — NVIDIA's own datasheet, SXM column\\n152|   (https://www.pny.com/File%20Library/Company/Support/Product%20Brochures/NVIDIA%20Data%20Center%20GPUs/english/nvidia-h100-datasheet.pdf).\\n153|\\n154|5. **H200 SXM keeps H100's compute die and power envelope but moves to 141 GB of HBM3e at 4.8 TB/s —\\n155|   1.4x H100's bandwidth, with identical compute throughput.** NVIDIA's page states both the capacity\\n156|   and the 1.4x relationship (https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/data-center/h200/).\\n157|\\n158|6. **B200 is a dual-die datacenter GPU with 180 GB of HBM3e at 7.7 TB/s and a TDP configurable up to\\n159|   1,000 W, on NVIDIA's custom TSMC 4NP process.** The Blackwell datasheet prints the per-GPU table;\\n160|   the architecture page states the transistor count and node\\n161|   (https://dam-cdn.nvd.orangelogic.com/AssetLink/y441155802qub41q118b2852i557jem5.pdf,\\n162|   https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/data-center/technologies/blackwell-architecture).\\n163|\\n164|7. **A100 80GB SXM4 was an early GPU to pass 2 TB/s: 80 GB of HBM2e at 2,039 GB/s at a 400 W TDP** —\\n165|   NVIDIA's A100 datasheet, 80GB SXM column\\n166|   (https://www.nvidia.com/content/dam/en-zz/Solutions/Data-Center/a100/pdf/nvidia-a100-datasheet-us-nvidia-1758950-r4-web.pdf).\\n167|\\n168|8. **Instinct MI300X carries 192 GB of HBM3 at \\\"5.3 TB/s max. peak theoretical\\\" over an 8,192-bit\\n169|   interface at 750 W maximum TBP** — the largest single-GPU memory pool in this dataset, from AMD's\\n170|   own data sheet\\n171|   (https://www.amd.com/content/dam/amd/en/documents/instinct-tech-docs/data-sheets/amd-instinct-mi300x-data-sheet.pdf).\\n172|\\n173|9. **Radeon RX 7900 XTX is a chiplet GPU: 24 GB of GDDR6 at 960 GB/s, 96 MB Infinity Cache, 355 W\\n174|   Typical Board Power, at an SEP of $999** — the price is stated in AMD's own launch press release\\n175|   (https://www.amd.com/en/products/graphics/desktops/radeon/7000-series/amd-radeon-rx-7900xtx.html,\\n176|   https://www.amd.com/en/newsroom/press-releases/2022-11-3-amd-unveils-world-s-most-advanced-gaming-graphics-.html).\\n177|\\n178|10. **Radeon RX 9070 XT is AMD's RDNA 4 flagship at an SEP of $599, with 16 GB GDDR6 at 640 GB/s and\\n179|    304 W Typical Board Power.** Unlike NVIDIA, AMD shipped no reference design this generation\\n180|    (https://www.amd.com/en/products/graphics/desktops/radeon/9000-series/amd-radeon-rx-9070xt.html).\\n181|\\n182|11. **Arc B580 ships 12 GB of GDDR6 at 456 GB/s on a 192-bit bus for a $249 recommended customer\\n183|    price at 190 W TBP, on TSMC N5** (https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/products/sku/241598/intel-arc-b580-graphics/specifications.html).\\n184|\\n185|12. **Arc A770 (16 GB) runs a 256-bit bus at 560 GB/s (17.5 Gbps) with 32 Xe-cores, 32 ray-tracing\\n186|    units and 512 XMX engines at 225 W TBP.** Intel's own desktop table lists the 16 GB and 8 GB A770\\n187|    at different bandwidths (560 vs 512 GB/s), so the two SKUs are not interchangeable\\n188|    (https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/products/sku/229151/intel-arc-a770-graphics-16gb/specifications.html).\\n189|\\n190|13. **Vendor AI figures are usually quoted with sparsity, and that roughly doubles them.** NVIDIA's\\n191|    H100 and H200 datasheets footnote their large tensor numbers \\\"with sparsity\\\" and state the values\\n192|    are \\\"1/2 lower without sparsity\\\" — hence 3,958 TOPS INT8 sparse versus 1,979 dense. NVIDIA's\\n193|    Blackwell datasheet says the same: \\\"Specifications in sparse… Dense is one-half of the sparse spec\\n194|    shown\\\". AMD's Radeon pages list dense and sparse separately (389 / 779 INT8 TOPS for the RX 9070\\n195|    XT). Intel labels its Arc figure simply \\\"GPU Peak TOPS (Int8)\\\". Cross-vendor \\\"TOPS\\\" comparisons\\n196|    are therefore invalid unless the sparsity basis is stated\\n197|    (https://www.pny.com/File%20Library/Company/Support/Product%20Brochures/NVIDIA%20Data%20Center%20GPUs/english/nvidia-h100-datasheet.pdf,\\n198|    https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/data-center/h200/,\\n199|    https://dam-cdn.nvd.orangelogic.com/AssetLink/y441155802qub41q118b2852i557jem5.pdf,\\n200|    https://www.amd.com/en/products/graphics/desktops/radeon/9000-series/amd-radeon-rx-9070xt.html).\\n201|\\n202|14. **The datacenter accelerators here have no ray-tracing hardware.** H100, H200 and A100 carry\\n203|    `rtCores: 0`; Hopper and Ampere datacenter silicon is a compute accelerator, not a graphics\\n204|    product. Their datasheets describe only compute and memory, with display outputs absent entirely\\n205|    (https://www.pny.com/File%20Library/Company/Support/Product%20Brochures/NVIDIA%20Data%20Center%20GPUs/english/nvidia-h100-datasheet.pdf,\\n206|    https://www.nvidia.com/content/dam/en-zz/Solutions/Data-Center/a100/pdf/nvidia-a100-datasheet-us-nvidia-1758950-r4-web.pdf).\\n207|\\n208|15. **NVIDIA's \\\"4N\\\" and \\\"4NP\\\" are not standard TSMC N4/N4P — they are NVIDIA-specific custom\\n209|    processes.** NVIDIA's Blackwell page states its GPUs \\\"are manufactured using a custom-built TSMC\\n210|    4NP process\\\"; the H100 and A100 datasheets publish no node name at all, so any pre-Blackwell node\\n211|    string is an inference from elsewhere, not from the vendor datasheet\\n212|    (https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/data-center/technologies/blackwell-architecture).\\n213|\\n214|---\\n215|\\n216|## Common myths / things people get wrong\\n217|\\n218|- **\\\"The RTX 5090 has twice the VRAM of the RTX 4090.\\\"** No — 32 GB against 24 GB is **33% more**,\\n219|  not 100% more. The *bandwidth* gap is larger (1,792 vs 1,008 GB/s, about 78% more).\\n220|\\n221|- **\\\"NVIDIA's AI TOPS numbers are INT8.\\\"** NVIDIA never labels the unit on its GeForce comparison\\n222|  page. Its headline numbers (3,352 / 1,801 / 988 for the 50-series) are sparse-basis figures, so\\n223|  placing them beside Intel's 233 (explicitly INT8) or AMD's 389 (dense INT8) produces a false\\n224|  comparison. Every `aiTops` value in `gpus.json` is dense and says so in `aiTopsUnit`.\\n225|\\n226|- **\\\"The B200 has 192 GB.\\\"** NVIDIA's own Blackwell datasheet prints **180 GB** for the HGX B200\\n227|  per-GPU memory, while a large body of secondary coverage repeats 192 GB. The two figures are a real\\n228|  vendor-vs-coverage discrepancy, not a typo: 192 GB is the physical stack size, 180 GB is what a\\n229|  workload can actually allocate. This dataset records 180 GB and flags the conflict rather than\\n230|  averaging the two. Marketing also rounds the 7.7 TB/s bandwidth up to \\\"8 TB/s\\\".\\n231|\\n232|- **\\\"Upgrading an H100 to an H200 gets you more compute.\\\"** H200 uses the **same GH100 compute die**:\\n233|  FP32 67 TFLOPS, FP16 1,979, FP8 3,958 and INT8 3,958 are identical between the two. Only the memory\\n234|  subsystem changed (80 GB HBM3/3.35 TB/s to 141 GB HBM3e/4.8 TB/s). Buying an H200 buys memory\\n235|  capacity and bandwidth, not FLOPS.\\n236|\\n237|- **\\\"A wider memory bus means more bandwidth.\\\"** Bus width alone determines nothing — bandwidth is\\n238|  bus width multiplied by per-pin data rate, and the memory *type* dominates. B200's 8,192-bit HBM3e\\n239|  at 7,700 GB/s and the RTX 5090's 512-bit GDDR7 at 1,792 GB/s are not comparable by bus width, and\\n240|  neither figure can be derived from the other without knowing the data rate. This dataset never\\n241|  derives bandwidth; each value is a published figure from the cited URL.\\n242|\\n243|- **\\\"The RX 7900 XTX has 3,500 GB/s of memory bandwidth.\\\"** AMD's page prints \\\"Effective Memory\\n244|  Bandwidth Up to 3500 GB/s\\\" — that is the Infinity Cache aggregate, not DRAM bandwidth. The GDDR6\\n245|  DRAM figure is **960 GB/s**, and that is what the dataset records. This one is a live trap on AMD's\\n246|  own product page.\\n247|\\n248|- **\\\"VRAM capacity is the same thing as VRAM speed.\\\"** MI300X has 192 GB at 5,300 GB/s; the RTX 5090\\n249|  has 32 GB at 1,792 GB/s. Capacity and bandwidth are independent axes: capacity limits model *size*,\\n250|  bandwidth limits token *rate*.\\n251|\\n252|- **\\\"Tensor cores replaced CUDA cores.\\\"** They are additive. The RTX 5090 still has 21,760 ordinary\\n253|  shader cores *plus* 680 tensor cores. Both counts appear in every applicable record here.\\n254|\\n255|- **\\\"TDP is how much power the card uses.\\\"** TDP/TGP/TBP is a **design ceiling**, not a measurement.\\n256|  Actual draw varies by workload and board partner. Note also that the same concept has three names:\\n257|  NVIDIA says TGP on consumer cards, AMD says \\\"Typical Board Power\\\", Intel says TBP.\\n258|\\n259|- **\\\"The RTX 5070's 12 GB and the RTX 5080's 16 GB are equally fine for local LLMs.\\\"** They are not\\n260|  the same constraint. Beyond bandwidth, capacity decides whether a model fits at all — a 4-bit 70B\\n261|  model needs roughly 35 GB, which fits on none of the consumer cards here and requires either a\\n262|  24 GB-plus card at aggressive quantisation or multi-GPU sharding.\\n263|\\n264|- **\\\"A 4 nm node is smaller than a 5 nm node in some physical sense you can measure.\\\"** \\\"4N\\\", \\\"4NP\\\",\\n265|  \\\"N4P\\\", \\\"N5\\\", \\\"N6\\\" are vendor *process names*, not gate lengths. NVIDIA's 4N and 4NP are custom TSMC\\n266|  variants; TSMC's \\\"N5\\\"/\\\"N4P\\\" and Intel's \\\"N5\\\"/\\\"N6\\\" are foundry marketing names. They are not\\n267|  comparable across foundries.\\n268|\\n269|---\\n270|\\n271|## Numbers I could NOT verify\\n272|\\n273|Everything below is `null` (or explicitly flagged) in `gpus.json`. Nothing was estimated, scaled,\\n274|interpolated or back-filled to fill a gap.\\n275|\\n276|- **`msrpUSD` = null for H100 SXM5, H200 SXM, B200, A100 80GB SXM4 and Instinct MI300X.** NVIDIA and\\n277|  AMD publish no list price for datacenter accelerators or Instinct parts. The only public numbers are\\n278|  street prices, OEM quotes and analyst estimates, none of which is a vendor MSRP.\\n279|\\n280|- **`msrpUSD` = null for the Arc A770 (16GB).** Intel's specification page carries no \\\"Recommended\\n281|  Customer Price\\\" row for the A770, unlike its B580 page which does print `$249.00`. The widely\\n282|  repeated $349 launch figure could not be confirmed from a primary source reachable in this run, so\\n283|  it is not recorded.\\n284|\\n285|- **`fp32TFLOPS`, `aiTops`, `tensorCores` and `rtCores` for the five GeForce cards are not\\n286|  vendor-published.** NVIDIA's GeForce spec tables state CUDA cores, memory, bandwidth, TGP, an\\n287|  unlabelled \\\"AI TOPS\\\" figure and RT TFLOPS, but no FP32 TFLOPS and no dense INT8 number. Those values\\n288|  come from the TechPowerUp GPU database entries for the exact SKUs. For the RTX 5090, RTX 4090 and\\n289|  RTX 4080 SUPER the TechPowerUp pages were fetched directly; for the **RTX 5080 and RTX 5070** the\\n290|  page had already gone behind the anti-bot wall, so the FP32/AI-TOPS/tensor/RT values were read from\\n291|  a search-engine rendering of the same canonical URL. That is why those two rows are `medium`.\\n292|\\n293|- **`aiTops` = null for the RTX 4080 SUPER — a deliberate gap.** NVIDIA publishes **836 AI TOPS** for\\n294|  it, but does not state whether that is dense or sparse, and no verified *dense* INT8 matrix figure\\n295|  was reachable (the TechPowerUp page for this SKU was fetched successfully, but its published table\\n296|  does not include a matrix-performance block, unlike the RTX 4090's). Rather than halve 836 on an\\n297|  assumption, the field is null and the 836 figure is recorded in the record's `notes`.\\n298|\\n299|- **`shaderCores` and `tensorCores` = null for B200.** NVIDIA's Blackwell datasheet publishes no\\n300|  CUDA-core or Tensor-core count per GPU. Third-party databases disagree with each other on the\\n301|  figure, so neither is recorded. Also **`busWidthBits` = null for B200**: NVIDIA does not publish a\\n302|  bus width, and computing 7.7 TB/s back into a bus width would be exactly the interpolation this\\n303|  dataset refuses to do.\\n304|\\n305|- **`rtCores` = null for B200 and MI300X** (as opposed to `0` for H100/H200/A100). The absence of\\n306|  ray-tracing hardware is documented for Hopper and Ampere datacenter parts, but for Blackwell\\n307|  datacenter and CDNA 3 no source I reached states the presence or absence of RT hardware, so the\\n308|  field is null rather than assumed to be zero.\\n309|\\n310|- **`busWidthBits`, `shaderCores` and `tensorCores` for H100 SXM5 (5,120 / 16,896 / 528) and\\n311|  H200 SXM (6,144 / 16,896 / 528) are NOT in NVIDIA's datasheets or product pages.** They were read\\n312|  from TechPowerUp GPU-database entries (`h100-sxm5-80-gb`, `h200-sxm-141-gb`) via search-engine\\n313|  renderings, because direct fetch of those pages returned 403 / the bot-check interstitial. The\\n314|  H200 per-GPU bandwidth of 4.8 TB/s *is* vendor-published; TechPowerUp's computed 4.89 TB/s for the\\n315|  same part is not used.\\n316|\\n317|- **`shaderCores` and `tensorCores` for A100 80GB SXM4 (6,912 / 432) are not in the A100 datasheet.**\\n318|  They come from **NVIDIA's own Ampere architecture whitepaper** (\\\"64 FP32 CUDA Cores/SM, 6912 FP32\\n319|  CUDA Cores per GPU… 4 Third-generation Tensor Cores/SM, 432 Third-generation Tensor Cores per GPU\\\"),\\n320|  but that PDF could not be fetched directly — its mirror returned the TechPowerUp bot wall's HTML —\\n321|  so it was read through a search-engine rendering. `busWidthBits` 5,120 for A100 comes from the same\\n322|  whitepaper's memory-interface line. This is why the A100 row is `medium` despite its other figures\\n323|  being first-hand vendor data.\\n324|\\n325|- **`processNode` for H100, H200 and the RTX 40/50-series rows is not printed in the documents used.**\\n326|  NVIDIA's H100 and A100 datasheets and its GeForce comparison page state no node name. \\\"TSMC 4N\\\" for\\n327|  those rows is the node NVIDIA associates with the architecture, not a value read off a vendor\\n328|  datasheet in this run. Only B200's \\\"TSMC 4NP\\\" is quoted from a vendor page\\n329|  (https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/data-center/technologies/blackwell-architecture).\\n330|\\n331|- **`fp32TFLOPS` = null for both Intel rows.** Intel publishes \\\"GPU Peak TOPS (Int8)\\\" for Arc but no\\n332|  FP32 TFLOPS rating. Deriving one would require assuming Intel's TOPS and any clock-based FP32\\n333|  estimate share a basis, which Intel does not state.\\n334|\\n335|- **`shaderCores` = null for both Intel rows.** Intel publishes Xe-core counts (20 for B580, 32 for\\n336|  A770), XMX-engine counts (160 / 512) and Xe Vector Engine counts (160 / 512) — but no \\\"shader\\n337|  cores\\\" count. Equating Xe Vector Engines with shader cores is a mapping Intel does not make, so the\\n338|  field is null and the published counts are in `notes`.\\n339|\\n340|- **No figure in this dataset is an independent measurement.** Every FLOPS/TOPS/TFLOPS value is a\\n341|  vendor theoretical peak, and every bandwidth and power figure is a vendor specification. Nothing\\n342|  was benchmarked in this run; a website using this dataset should present these as *specifications*,\\n343|  never as measured performance.\\n344|\\n345|---\\n346|\\n347|## Background: the CPU-vs-GPU architectural difference\\n348|\\n349|A CPU is engineered to minimise **latency** on a single instruction stream. Most of its silicon goes\\n350|to large caches, branch predictors, out-of-order execution and speculative scheduling, serving a\\n351|handful of very fast, heavily-optimised cores (typically 8-64). A GPU is engineered to maximise\\n352|**throughput** across thousands of independent instruction streams: hundreds or thousands of simple\\n353|cores, very little per-core control logic, and memory latency hidden by switching between resident\\n354|threads rather than by predicting. This is why a GPU can process vastly more parallel arithmetic per\\n355|second while running at a much lower clock than a CPU — but only when the problem is thousands of\\n356|independent, uniform operations over large blocks of data (matrix maths, physics, hashing, rendering).\\n357|Serial, branch-heavy or pointer-chasing work runs *badly* on a GPU, and that is the whole reason the\\n358|two coexist rather than one replacing the other. The practical consequence is visible in this\\n359|dataset's own hardware: a datacenter GPU is never deployed alone. AMD's MI300X data sheet describes\\n360|memory \\\"shared coherently between CPUs and GPUs\\\", and NVIDIA's H200 page attributes its headline HPC\\n361|speed-up to pairing GPUs with host CPUs — the CPU still owns control flow, I/O and everything that\\n362|cannot be parallelised\\n363|(https://www.amd.com/content/dam/amd/en/documents/instinct-tech-docs/data-sheets/amd-instinct-mi300x-data-sheet.pdf,\\n364|https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/data-center/h200/,\\n365|https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CUDA).\\n366|\\n367|## Background: why tensor cores exist, and how they differ from CUDA cores\\n368|\\n369|CUDA/shader cores are **general-purpose scalar lanes**. Each executes one FP32 or INT32 operation per\\n370|clock per lane and can handle arbitrary arithmetic, branching, addressing and graphics shading — they\\n371|are flexible but they do one multiply-accumulate at a time per lane. Tensor cores are\\n372|**fixed-function matrix units**: they exist because deep learning is overwhelmingly dominated by a\\n373|single operation, the matrix multiply-accumulate (dense GEMM), and running GEMMs on scalar lanes\\n374|wastes most of the silicon on instruction issue rather than arithmetic. A tensor core performs a\\n375|small matrix multiply-accumulate in hardware — a whole tile of values per instruction — at reduced\\n376|precision.\\n377|\\n378|The generational story is almost entirely about *which precisions* can be fed into that unit, because\\n379|lower precision means more throughput per unit of silicon. NVIDIA's H100 datasheet frames its\\n380|4th-generation tensor cores together with a \\\"Transformer Engine with FP8 precision\\\"; the Blackwell\\n381|datasheet introduces FP4/FP6 and a second-generation Transformer Engine with microscaling formats and\\n382|states the per-GPU figures across precisions (FP4 18 PFLOPS, FP8/FP6 9 PFLOPS, FP16/BF16 4.5 PFLOPS,\\n383|FP32 75 TFLOPS — all sparse except FP32), which is a direct illustration of how steeply throughput\\n384|rises as precision falls. AMD's equivalent story runs through its Matrix Cores on CDNA 3\\n385|(1,216 matrix cores on MI300X) and its \\\"AI Accelerators\\\" on RDNA 4, and Intel's through XMX engines.\\n386|\\n387|The payoff is order-of-magnitude: the RTX 5090's 680 tensor cores are rated at 838 dense INT8 TOPS\\n388|against 104.8 FP32 TFLOPS from its 21,760 shader cores — roughly eight times the throughput on that\\n389|specific workload. Tensor cores cannot run a game's shaders, a sort, or a branch-heavy kernel; they\\n390|accelerate matmuls at the precisions they support and nothing else. That is precisely why both core\\n391|types coexist in every GPU in this dataset rather than one replacing the other\\n392|(https://www.pny.com/File%20Library/Company/Support/Product%20Brochures/NVIDIA%20Data%20Center%20GPUs/english/nvidia-h100-datasheet.pdf,\\n393|https://dam-cdn.nvd.orangelogic.com/AssetLink/y441155802qub41q118b2852i557jem5.pdf,\\n394|https://www.techpowerup.com/gpu-specs/geforce-rtx-5090.c4216,\\n395|https://www.amd.com/content/dam/amd/en/documents/instinct-tech-docs/data-sheets/amd-instinct-mi300x-data-sheet.pdf).\\n396|\\n397|## GPU generation timeline, 2006 -> 2025\\n398|\\n399|| Year | Architecture (NVIDIA unless noted) | Notable change | Source |\\n400||---|---|---|---|\\n401|| 2006 | Tesla / G80 (GeForce 8800 GTX) | First unified shader architecture — one shader core type for both vertex and pixel work, replacing separate vertex/pixel pipelines; the hardware generation CUDA was written for | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CUDA |\\n402|| 2008 | Tesla / GT200 | Double-precision (FP64) arithmetic added in a consumer-class GPU | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CUDA |\\n403|| 2010 | Fermi (GF100) | Compute-first design: ECC memory, usable L1/L2 caches, full C++ support — the point where GPGPU became practical | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CUDA |\\n404|| 2012 | Kepler (GK104) | Efficiency focus; dynamic parallelism (kernels launching kernels) | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CUDA |\\n405|| 2014 | Maxwell (GM204) | Performance-per-watt redesign; larger L2 per core | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CUDA |\\n406|| 2016 | Pascal (GP100 / GP104) | 16 nm finFET and HBM2 on the datacenter part — the first generation NVIDIA lists as a GeForce architecture step to the modern era | https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/geforce/graphics-cards/compare/ |\\n407|| 2017 | Volta (GV100) | **First dedicated Tensor Cores** — hardware matrix multiply-accumulate for deep learning | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CUDA |\\n408|| 2018 | Turing (TU102) | **RT cores** arrive (1st generation); NVIDIA's own table lists the RTX 20 Series as \\\"Turing, 1st Generation\\\" RT and \\\"2nd Generation\\\" Tensor Cores | https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/geforce/graphics-cards/compare/ |\\n409|| 2020 | Ampere (GA100 datacenter / GA10x consumer) | 3rd-generation Tensor Cores; A100 80GB pushes HBM2e past 2 TB/s | https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/geforce/graphics-cards/compare/ |\\n410|| 2022 | Hopper (GH100) + Ada Lovelace (AD102); AMD RDNA 3 | Hopper brings FP8 and the Transformer Engine; Ada brings 4th-generation Tensor Cores and 3rd-generation RT cores to consumers. AMD's RDNA 3 is the first chiplet-based consumer GPU (RX 7900 XTX) | https://www.pny.com/File%20Library/Company/Support/Product%20Brochures/NVIDIA%20Data%20Center%20GPUs/english/nvidia-h100-datasheet.pdf, https://www.amd.com/en/products/graphics/desktops/radeon/7000-series/amd-radeon-rx-7900xtx.html |\\n411|| 2023 | AMD CDNA 3 (MI300X) | 192 GB of HBM3 at 5.3 TB/s in one OAM module — a capacity-led design aimed squarely at LLM inference | https://www.amd.com/content/dam/amd/en/documents/instinct-tech-docs/data-sheets/amd-instinct-mi300x-data-sheet.pdf |\\n412|| 2024 | Blackwell datacenter (B200) + Xe2 (Arc B580) | B200: 5th-generation Tensor Cores, native FP4/FP6, dual-die package on TSMC 4NP, 180 GB HBM3e. Intel's Battlemage (Xe2) arrives in December | https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/data-center/technologies/blackwell-architecture, https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/products/sku/241598/intel-arc-b580-graphics/specifications.html |\\n413|| 2025 | GeForce RTX 50 series (Blackwell) + AMD RDNA 4 (RX 9000) | GDDR7 on the consumer flagship — 1,792 GB/s on the RTX 5090, up from 1,008 GB/s on the RTX 4090; 5th-generation Tensor Cores and 4th-generation RT cores on the consumer parts; AMD's RDNA 4 moves to 2nd-generation AI accelerators | https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/geforce/graphics-cards/compare/, https://www.amd.com/en/products/graphics/desktops/radeon/9000-series/amd-radeon-rx-9070xt.html |\\n414|\\n415|Two honesty notes on this table. First, entries from 2006 to 2017 rest on Wikipedia's CUDA and\\n416|architecture articles, which are **secondary** sources; the 2016-to-2025 rows are anchored in NVIDIA's\\n417|own comparison table, NVIDIA's datasheets and AMD's product pages, which are primary. Second, the\\n418|table is NVIDIA-centric because that is where the primary sources I could reach are strongest — AMD's\\n419|parallel track (GCN 2011, RDNA 2019, RDNA 2 2020, RDNA 3 2022, RDNA 4 2025) and Intel's (Arc\\n420|Alchemist 2022, Battlemage 2024) are only partially represented, with the AMD and Intel entries above\\n421|covering the points their own product pages support.\\n422|\\n423|## What VRAM bandwidth means for LLM inference\\n424|\\n425|LLM inference is two phases with different bottlenecks. **Prefill** — processing the prompt — is\\n426|compute-bound: it is effectively one large batched matmul, so tensor-core throughput and the\\n427|precisions supported (FP8, FP4) set the speed. **Decode** — generating one token at a time — is\\n428|**memory-bandwidth-bound**: to produce each single token, essentially every model weight must be\\n429|streamed out of VRAM through the compute units, and the amount of arithmetic performed per byte read\\n430|is tiny. When the arithmetic-per-byte ratio is that low, the GPU spends its time waiting on memory\\n431|rather than computing, so token rate scales with memory bandwidth.\\n432|\\n433|Why vendors position parts the way they do follows directly. The H200 exists despite having the\\n434|*identical* compute die to the H100, because its 4.8 TB/s (versus 3.35 TB/s) matters on memory-bound\\n435|decode; NVIDIA's own framing is that \\\"the H200's larger and faster memory accelerates generative AI\\n436|and LLMs\\\", and the same page claims 1.9x higher Llama2 70B inference throughput versus H100\\n437|(https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/data-center/h200/). AMD positions MI300X's 192 GB HBM3 at 5.3 TB/s as\\n438|\\\"shared coherently between CPUs and GPUs\\\" for exactly the same reason\\n439|(https://www.amd.com/content/dam/amd/en/documents/instinct-tech-docs/data-sheets/amd-instinct-mi300x-data-sheet.pdf).\\n440|NVIDIA's B200 datasheet likewise lists memory capacity and bandwidth as first-class per-GPU\\n441|specifications alongside the FLOPS figures.\\n442|\\n443|A useful way to state the relationship — labelled here as arithmetic, *not* as a vendor claim or a\\n444|measured result — is that single-stream decode token rate is bounded roughly by `memory bandwidth ÷\\n445|model size in bytes`, and is further cut by KV-cache traffic. A 70B-parameter model in 4-bit weights\\n446|is about 35 GB, so at 1,792 GB/s (RTX 5090) the arithmetic ceiling is on the order of 50 tokens/s,\\n447|while a 5.3 TB/s MI300X streaming the same 35 GB has a ceiling around 150 tokens/s. Real throughput\\n448|is below these figures because of compute limits, batching, KV-cache reads and framework overhead.\\n449|\\n450|The second, independent axis is **capacity**. If the model plus KV cache does not fit in VRAM, it\\n451|does not run at all without sharding across multiple GPUs — which is why 192 GB (MI300X), 180 GB\\n452|(B200) and 141 GB (H200) matter for large models in a way raw TFLOPS does not. Capacity decides\\n453|whether a model fits; bandwidth decides how fast it decodes once it does. Neither substitutes for the\\n454|other.\\n455|\\n456|---\\n457|\\n458|## Glossary (10 terms)\\n459|\\n460|1. **GPU (graphics processing unit)** — a throughput-oriented processor with hundreds or thousands of\\n461|   simple cores, built to hide memory latency by switching between many parallel threads rather than\\n462|   by predicting branches (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CUDA).\\n463|\\n464|2. **VRAM** — the GPU's own memory, separate from system RAM. Its *capacity* sets the largest model,\\n465|   texture set or framebuffer that fits; its *type* (GDDR6, GDDR6X, GDDR7, HBM3, HBM3e) largely\\n466|   determines the achievable bandwidth.\\n467|\\n468|3. **Shader core / CUDA core / stream processor** — the general-purpose scalar execution lane: one\\n469|   per-lane arithmetic op per clock, and the only place arbitrary maths, branches and graphics\\n470|   shading can run. Recorded as `shaderCores` here (NVIDIA \\\"CUDA cores\\\", AMD \\\"stream processors\\\").\\n471|   Intel publishes no equivalent count for Arc, so those rows are null.\\n472|\\n473|4. **Tensor core / AI accelerator / matrix core / XMX engine** — a fixed-function matrix\\n474|   multiply-accumulate unit for deep-learning GEMMs. Exists only to accelerate matmuls at specific\\n475|   low precisions; useless for branching code. Recorded as `tensorCores` here.\\n476|\\n477|5. **RT core / ray accelerator / ray-tracing unit** — fixed-function hardware for ray-triangle\\n478|   intersection and BVH traversal. Present on consumer GPUs; **absent (recorded as 0)** on every\\n479|   Hopper and Ampere datacenter part in this dataset.\\n480|\\n481|6. **TFLOPS** — trillions (10^12) of floating-point operations per second. In this dataset always a\\n482|   vendor *theoretical peak* from cores x ops-per-clock x clock, never a measured benchmark.\\n483|\\n484|7. **TOPS** — trillions of (usually integer) operations per second. The trap: a figure doubles if\\n485|   2:4 structured sparsity is enabled, and vendors are inconsistent about saying so. `aiTops` in this\\n486|   dataset is always dense and `aiTopsUnit` states the precision.\\n487|\\n488|8. **HBM (high bandwidth memory)** — DRAM stacked in layers and connected through a very wide\\n489|   interface (5,120-bit on H100/A100, 6,144-bit on H200, 8,192-bit on MI300X) directly beside the\\n490|   die, giving terabytes per second at high cost and high power. Consumer cards use narrower GDDR\\n491|   instead, which is why a 512-bit GDDR7 consumer card cannot match a 5,120-bit HBM part.\\n492|\\n493|9. **Memory bandwidth** — the rate at which bytes move between VRAM and the compute units, in GB/s\\n494|   here. Always taken from a published figure in this dataset, never derived from bus width and\\n495|   memory type. It is the binding constraint on LLM decode; see the section above.\\n496|\\n497|10. **TDP / TGP / TBP** — thermal design power / total graphics power / typical board power: the\\n498|    board's **design power ceiling**, not a measurement of consumption. NVIDIA uses TGP on consumer\\n499|    cards and TDP on datacenter parts; AMD uses \\\"Typical Board Power\\\"; Intel uses TBP\\n500|    (https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/geforce/graphics-cards/50-series/rtx-5090/,\\n501|    https://www.amd.com/en/products/graphics/desktops/radeon/7000-series/amd-radeon-rx-7900xtx.html,\\n502|    https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/products/sku/241598/intel-arc-b580-graphics/specifications.html).\", \"total_lines\": 502, \"file_size\": 38461, \"truncated\": false, \"is_binary\": false, \"is_image\": false, \"not_found\": false}", "tool_call_id": "call-9bdebea2-b1d2-4d36-84c5-ea88759d5fd3", "tool_calls": null, "tool_name": "read_file", "effect_disposition": null, "timestamp": 1790791170.1586926, "token_count": null, "finish_reason": null, "reasoning": null, "reasoning_content": null, "reasoning_details": null, "codex_reasoning_items": null, "codex_message_items": null, "platform_message_id": null, "observed": 0, "active": 1, "compacted": 0, "api_content": null, "display_kind": null, "display_metadata": null, "message_uid": "b2820ecf4a774961a13bd8a5e8ff5f56", "absorbed_message_uids": null, "tool_call_uids": null, "tool_call_uid": "2d993d08bd894e118cf7e6aefcaee758"}, {"id": 244, "session_id": "20260930_225432_76ed87", "role": "assistant", "content": "", "tool_call_id": null, "tool_calls": [{"id": "call-8f551bb3-d7d9-48a0-9848-46e51f42c23a", "call_id": "call-8f551bb3-d7d9-48a0-9848-46e51f42c23a", "response_item_id": "fc_call-8f551bb3-d7d9-48a0-9848-46e51f42c23a", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "read_file", "arguments": "{\"path\":\"/home/sudhanshunaik/Projects/gpu-website/index.html\"}"}}, {"id": "call-c252f5d0-ce5b-49e9-8b75-bd94a514daf7", "call_id": "call-c252f5d0-ce5b-49e9-8b75-bd94a514daf7", "response_item_id": "fc_call-c252f5d0-ce5b-49e9-8b75-bd94a514daf7", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "read_file", "arguments": "{\"path\":\"/home/sudhanshunaik/Projects/gpu-website/script.js\",\"limit\":100}"}}], "tool_name": null, "effect_disposition": null, "timestamp": 1790791187.9552894, "token_count": null, "finish_reason": "tool_calls", "reasoning": "E