ent\\\">Atlas</span></span>\\n38|    </a>\\n39|\\n40|    <nav class=\\\"main-nav\\\" id=\\\"main-nav\\\" aria-label=\\\"Section navigation\\\">\\n41|      <ul>\\n42|        <li><a href=\\\"#cpu-vs-gpu\\\">GPU vs CPU</a></li>\\n43|        <li><a href=\\\"#inside\\\">Inside</a></li>\\n44|        <li><a href=\\\"#compare\\\">Compare</a></li>\\n45|        <li><a href=\\\"#use-cases\\\">Use cases</a></li>\\n46|        <li><a href=\\\"#timeline\\\">Timeline</a></li>\\n47|        <li><a href=\\\"#faq\\\">FAQ</a></li>\\n48|      </ul>\\n49|    </nav>\\n50|\\n51|    <button type=\\\"button\\\" class=\\\"theme-toggle\\\" id=\\\"theme-toggle\\\" aria-pressed=\\\"false\\\">\\n52|      <span class=\\\"theme-toggle-label\\\" id=\\\"theme-toggle-label\\\">Switch to light theme</span>\\n53|      <svg class=\\\"icon-moon\\\" viewBox=\\\"0 0 24 24\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\" focusable=\\\"false\\\">\\n54|        <path d=\\\"M20.5 14.5A8.5 8.5 0 0 1 9.5 3.5a8.5 8.5 0 1 0 11 11Z\\\" fill=\\\"currentColor\\\"></path>\\n55|      </svg>\\n56|      <svg class=\\\"icon-sun\\\" viewBox=\\\"0 0 24 24\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\" focusable=\\\"false\\\">\\n57|        <circle cx=\\\"12\\\" cy=\\\"12\\\" r=\\\"4.2\\\" fill=\\\"currentColor\\\"></circle>\\n58|        <path d=\\\"M12 2.5v2.6M12 18.9v2.6M4.3 4.3l1.9 1.9M17.8 17.8l1.9 1.9M2.5 12h2.6M18.9 12h2.6M4.3 19.7l1.9-1.9M17.8 6.2l1.9-1.9\\\" stroke=\\\"currentColor\\\" stroke-width=\\\"1.9\\\" stroke-linecap=\\\"round\\\"></path>\\n59|      </svg>\\n60|    </button>\\n61|  </div>\\n62|</header>\\n63|\\n64|<main id=\\\"main\\\">\\n65|\\n66|  <!-- ============================ HERO ============================ -->\\n67|  <section id=\\\"top\\\" class=\\\"section hero\\\" aria-labelledby=\\\"hero-title\\\">\\n68|    <div class=\\\"wrap hero-grid\\\">\\n69|      <div class=\\\"hero-copy\\\">\\n70|        <p class=\\\"eyebrow\\\">Graphics processing units, explained</p>\\n71|        <h1 id=\\\"hero-title\\\">Thousands of small cores drawing <span class=\\\"grad\\\">one big picture</span></h1>\\n72|        <p class=\\\"lede\\\">\\n73|          A GPU is not a faster CPU. It is an entirely different bet on how to spend silicon:\\n74|          give up per-task cleverness, buy enormous parallelism, and win wherever the same\\n75|          instruction applies to a million different pieces of data — pixels, vertices, matrix\\n76|          rows, particles. This page walks through how that works, what lives inside the chip,\\n77|          and how 15 real GPUs from 2016&ndash;2025 compare on the numbers vendors actually publish.\\n78|        </p>\\n79|        <div class=\\\"hero-actions\\\">\\n80|          <a class=\\\"btn btn-primary\\\" href=\\\"#compare\\\">Compare 15 GPUs</a>\\n81|          <a class=\\\"btn btn-ghost\\\" href=\\\"#inside\\\">Look inside the chip</a>\\n82|        </div>\\n83|      </div>\\n84|\\n85|      <div class=\\\"hero-visual\\\">\\n86|        <svg class=\\\"die-svg\\\" viewBox=\\\"0 0 760 460\\\" role=\\\"img\\\" aria-labelledby=\\\"dieTitle dieDesc\\\">\\n87|          <title id=\\\"dieTitle\\\">Diagram of a GPU package and its die</title>\\n88|          <desc id=\\\"dieDesc\\\">A graphics processor package: a central die filled with a grid of shader cores grouped into streaming multiprocessors, surrounded by on-package memory chips connected over a wide memory bus. A highlight sweeps across the core grid.</desc>\\n89|          <defs>\\n90|            <linearGradient id=\\\"pkgGrad\\\" x1=\\\"0\\\" y1=\\\"0\\\" x2=\\\"1\\\" y2=\\\"1\\\">\\n91|              <stop offset=\\\"0\\\" stop-color=\\\"#1d2233\\\"></stop>\\n92|              <stop offset=\\\"0.55\\\" stop-color=\\\"#141824\\\"></stop>\\n93|              <stop offset=\\\"1\\\" stop-color=\\\"#0f1320\\\"></stop>\\n94|            </linearGradient>\\n95|            <linearGradient id=\\\"dieGrad\\\" x1=\\\"0\\\" y1=\\\"0\\\" x2=\\\"1\\\" y2=\\\"1\\\">\\n96|              <stop offset=\\\"0\\\" stop-color=\\\"#111524\\\"></stop>\\n97|              <stop offset=\\\"1\\\" stop-color=\\\"#0b0e18\\\"></stop>\\n98|            </linearGradient>\\n99|            <linearGradient id=\\\"coreGrad\\\" x1=\\\"0\\\" y1=\\\"0\\\" x2=\\\"1\\\" y2=\\\"1\\\">\\n100|              <stop offset=\\\"0\\\" stop-color=\\\"#7c5cff\\\"></stop>\\n101|              <stop offset=\\\"1\\\" stop-color=\\\"#22d3ee\\\"></stop>\\n102|            </linearGradient>\\n103|            <linearGradient id=\\\"sweepGrad\\\" x1=\\\"0\\\" y1=\\\"0\\\" x2=\\\"1\\\" y2=\\\"0\\\">\\n104|              <stop offset=\\\"0\\\" stop-color=\\\"#22d3ee\\\" stop-opacity=\\\"0\\\"></stop>\\n105|              <stop offset=\\\"0.5\\\" stop-color=\\\"#9b8cff\\\" stop-opacity=\\\"0.55\\\"></stop>\\n106|              <stop offset=\\\"1\\\" stop-color=\\\"#22d3ee\\\" stop-opacity=\\\"0\\\"></stop>\\n107|            </linearGradient>\\n108|            <pattern id=\\\"corePattern\\\" width=\\\"24\\\" height=\\\"24\\\" patternUnits=\\\"userSpaceOnUse\\\">\\n109|              <rect x=\\\"3\\\" y=\\\"3\\\" width=\\\"18\\\" height=\\\"18\\\" rx=\\\"4\\\" fill=\\\"url(#coreGrad)\\\" opacity=\\\"0.8\\\"></rect>\\n110|            </pattern>\\n111|            <clipPath id=\\\"dieClip\\\"><rect x=\\\"200\\\" y=\\\"88\\\" width=\\\"360\\\" height=\\\"284\\\" rx=\\\"16\\\"></rect></clipPath>\\n112|          </defs>\\n113|\\n114|          <!-- package and pins -->\\n115|          <rect class=\\\"pkg\\\" x=\\\"26\\\" y=\\\"26\\\" width=\\\"708\\\" height=\\\"408\\\" rx=\\\"26\\\"></rect>\\n116|          <g class=\\\"pins\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\"></g>\\n117|\\n118|          <!-- on-package memory chips -->\\n119|          <g class=\\\"mem-chips\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\">\\n120|            <rect x=\\\"62\\\" y=\\\"120\\\" width=\\\"112\\\" height=\\\"56\\\" rx=\\\"8\\\"></rect>\\n121|            <rect x=\\\"62\\\" y=\\\"188\\\" width=\\\"112\\\" height=\\\"56\\\" rx=\\\"8\\\"></rect>\\n122|            <rect x=\\\"62\\\" y=\\\"256\\\" width=\\\"112\\\" height=\\\"56\\\" rx=\\\"8\\\"></rect>\\n123|            <rect x=\\\"586\\\" y=\\\"120\\\" width=\\\"112\\\" height=\\\"56\\\" rx=\\\"8\\\"></rect>\\n124|            <rect x=\\\"586\\\" y=\\\"188\\\" width=\\\"112\\\" height=\\\"56\\\" rx=\\\"8\\\"></rect>\\n125|            <rect x=\\\"586\\\" y=\\\"256\\\" width=\\\"112\\\" height=\\\"56\\\" rx=\\\"8\\\"></rect>\\n126|          </g>\\n127|\\n128|          <!-- memory bus traces -->\\n129|          <g class=\\\"bus\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\">\\n130|            <path d=\\\"M174 148H200M174 216H200M174 284H200\\\"></path>\\n131|            <path d=\\\"M560 148h26M560 216h26M560 284h26\\\"></path>\\n132|          </g>\\n133|\\n134|          <rect class=\\\"die\\\" x=\\\"200\\\" y=\\\"88\\\" width=\\\"360\\\" height=\\\"284\\\"></rect>\\n135|\\n136|          <g clip-path=\\\"url(#dieClip)\\\">\\n137|            <rect x=\\\"212\\\" y=\\\"100\\\" width=\\\"336\\\" height=\\\"260\\\" fill=\\\"url(#corePattern)\\\"></rect>\\n138|            <rect class=\\\"sweep\\\" x=\\\"-180\\\" y=\\\"88\\\" width=\\\"180\\\" height=\\\"284\\\" fill=\\\"url(#sweepGrad)\\\"></rect>\\n139|          </g>\\n140|\\n141|          <!-- streaming multiprocessor boundaries -->\\n142|          <g class=\\\"sm-boundaries\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\">\\n143|            <rect x=\\\"208\\\" y=\\\"96\\\" width=\\\"168\\\" height=\\\"128\\\" rx=\\\"10\\\"></rect>\\n144|            <rect x=\\\"384\\\" y=\\\"96\\\" width=\\\"168\\\" height=\\\"128\\\" rx=\\\"10\\\"></rect>\\n145|            <rect x=\\\"208\\\" y=\\\"236\\\" width=\\\"168\\\" height=\\\"128\\\" rx=\\\"10\\\"></rect>\\n146|            <rect x=\\\"384\\\" y=\\\"236\\\" width=\\\"168\\\" height=\\\"128\\\" rx=\\\"10\\\"></rect>\\n147|          </g>\\n148|\\n149|          <g class=\\\"svg-labels\\\">\\n150|            <text class=\\\"svg-label\\\" x=\\\"118\\\" y=\\\"108\\\" text-anchor=\\\"middle\\\">VRAM</text>\\n151|            <text class=\\\"svg-label\\\" x=\\\"642\\\" y=\\\"108\\\" text-anchor=\\\"middle\\\">VRAM</text>\\n152|            <text class=\\\"svg-label sm-label\\\" x=\\\"292\\\" y=\\\"118\\\" text-anchor=\\\"middle\\\">SM</text>\\n153|            <text class=\\\"svg-label sm-label\\\" x=\\\"468\\\" y=\\\"118\\\" text-anchor=\\\"middle\\\">SM</text>\\n154|            <text class=\\\"svg-label sm-label\\\" x=\\\"292\\\" y=\\\"258\\\" text-anchor=\\\"middle\\\">SM</text>\\n155|            <text class=\\\"svg-label sm-label\\\" x=\\\"468\\\" y=\\\"258\\\" text-anchor=\\\"middle\\\">SM</text>\\n156|            <text class=\\\"svg-label bus-label\\\" x=\\\"380\\\" y=\\\"418\\\" text-anchor=\\\"middle\\\">memory bus &mdash; hundreds to thousands of bits wide</text>\\n157|          </g>\\n158|        </svg>\\n159|      </div>\\n160|    </div>\\n161|\\n162|    <div class=\\\"wrap\\\">\\n163|      <h2 class=\\\"visually-hidden\\\" id=\\\"hero-stats-title\\\">Comparison table at a glance</h2>\\n164|      <ul class=\\\"stat-tiles\\\" id=\\\"hero-stats\\\" aria-labelledby=\\\"hero-stats-title\\\">\\n165|        <!-- Filled by script.js from the GPUS array. -->\\n166|      </ul>\\n167|    </div>\\n168|  </section>\\n169|\\n170|  <!-- ========================= GPU VS CPU ========================= -->\\n171|  <section id=\\\"cpu-vs-gpu\\\" class=\\\"section\\\" aria-labelledby=\\\"cpuvgpu-title\\\">\\n172|    <div class=\\\"wrap\\\">\\n173|      <p class=\\\"eyebrow\\\">The core idea</p>\\n174|      <h2 id=\\\"cpuvgpu-title\\\">GPU vs CPU: latency versus throughput</h2>\\n175|      <p class=\\\"section-lede\\\">\\n176|        A modern CPU core is a marvel of cheating time: branch prediction, out-of-order execution,\\n177|        huge caches and speculative work so that one instruction stream finishes as fast as\\n178|        physically possible. That machinery is expensive in area and power, which is why you get\\n179|        eight to twenty-four of those cores in a desktop socket. A GPU spends the same silicon\\n180|        differently &mdash; thousands of tiny cores that are individually slow but never idle,\\n181|        because the work is embarrassingly parallel.\\n182|      </p>\\n183|\\n184|      <div class=\\\"split\\\">\\n185|        <div class=\\\"split-visual\\\">\\n186|          <svg class=\\\"cpu-svg\\\" viewBox=\\\"0 0 900 430\\\" role=\\\"img\\\" aria-labelledby=\\\"cpuTitle cpuDesc\\\">\\n187|            <title id=\\\"cpuTitle\\\">CPU cores next to GPU cores</title>\\n188|            <desc id=\\\"cpuDesc\\\">On the left, a CPU with four large complex cores, each paired with its own control logic and cache. On the right, a GPU with dozens of much smaller simple cores that share control logic.</desc>\\n189|            <defs>\\n190|              <linearGradient id=\\\"gpuCoreGrad\\\" x1=\\\"0\\\" y1=\\\"0\\\" x2=\\\"1\\\" y2=\\\"1\\\">\\n191|                <stop offset=\\\"0\\\" stop-color=\\\"#7c5cff\\\"></stop>\\n192|                <stop offset=\\\"1\\\" stop-color=\\\"#22d3ee\\\"></stop>\\n193|              </linearGradient>\\n194|              <pattern id=\\\"manyCorePattern\\\" width=\\\"26\\\" height=\\\"26\\\" patternUnits=\\\"userSpaceOnUse\\\">\\n195|                <rect x=\\\"3\\\" y=\\\"3\\\" width=\\\"20\\\" height=\\\"20\\\" rx=\\\"4\\\" fill=\\\"url(#gpuCoreGrad)\\\" opacity=\\\"0.85\\\"></rect>\\n196|              </pattern>\\n197|            </defs>\\n198|\\n199|            <g class=\\\"panel\\\">\\n200|              <rect class=\\\"panel-bg\\\" x=\\\"16\\\" y=\\\"16\\\" width=\\\"404\\\" height=\\\"398\\\" rx=\\\"18\\\"></rect>\\n201|              <text class=\\\"panel-title\\\" x=\\\"42\\\" y=\\\"58\\\">CPU</text>\\n202|              <text class=\\\"panel-sub\\\" x=\\\"42\\\" y=\\\"82\\\">4&ndash;24 very complex cores</text>\\n203|\\n204|              <g class=\\\"cpu-core\\\">\\n205|                <rect x=\\\"42\\\" y=\\\"102\\\" width=\\\"352\\\" height=\\\"62\\\" rx=\\\"10\\\"></rect>\\n206|                <text class=\\\"core-text\\\" x=\\\"62\\\" y=\\\"139\\\">Core 1</text>\\n207|                <rect class=\\\"core-sub\\\" x=\\\"150\\\" y=\\\"116\\\" width=\\\"112\\\" height=\\\"34\\\" rx=\\\"6\\\"></rect>\\n208|                <text class=\\\"core-subtext\\\" x=\\\"206\\\" y=\\\"138\\\" text-anchor=\\\"middle\\\">control</text>\\n209|                <rect class=\\\"core-sub\\\" x=\\\"272\\\" y=\\\"116\\\" width=\\\"106\\\" height=\\\"34\\\" rx=\\\"6\\\"></rect>\\n210|                <text class=\\\"core-subtext\\\" x=\\\"325\\\" y=\\\"138\\\" text-anchor=\\\"middle\\\">cache</text>\\n211|              </g>\\n212|              <g class=\\\"cpu-core\\\">\\n213|                <rect x=\\\"42\\\" y=\\\"176\\\" width=\\\"352\\\" height=\\\"62\\\" rx=\\\"10\\\"></rect>\\n214|                <text class=\\\"core-text\\\" x=\\\"62\\\" y=\\\"213\\\">Core 2</text>\\n215|                <rect class=\\\"core-sub\\\" x=\\\"150\\\" y=\\\"190\\\" width=\\\"112\\\" height=\\\"34\\\" rx=\\\"6\\\"></rect>\\n216|                <text class=\\\"core-subtext\\\" x=\\\"206\\\" y=\\\"212\\\" text-anchor=\\\"middle\\\">control</text>\\n217|                <rect class=\\\"core-sub\\\" x=\\\"272\\\" y=\\\"190\\\" width=\\\"106\\\" height=\\\"34\\\" rx=\\\"6\\\"></rect>\\n218|                <text class=\\\"core-subtext\\\" x=\\\"325\\\" y=\\\"212\\\" text-anchor=\\\"middle\\\">cache</text>\\n219|              </g>\\n220|              <g class=\\\"cpu-core\\\">\\n221|                <rect x=\\\"42\\\" y=\\\"250\\\" width=\\\"352\\\" height=\\\"62\\\" rx=\\\"10\\\"></rect>\\n222|                <text class=\\\"core-text\\\" x=\\\"62\\\" y=\\\"287\\\">Core 3</text>\\n223|                <rect class=\\\"core-sub\\\" x=\\\"150\\\" y=\\\"264\\\" width=\\\"112\\\" height=\\\"34\\\" rx=\\\"6\\\"></rect>\\n224|                <text class=\\\"core-subtext\\\" x=\\\"206\\\" y=\\\"286\\\" text-anchor=\\\"middle\\\">control</text>\\n225|                <rect class=\\\"core-sub\\\" x=\\\"272\\\" y=\\\"264\\\" width=\\\"106\\\" height=\\\"34\\\" rx=\\\"6\\\"></rect>\\n226|                <text class=\\\"core-subtext\\\" x=\\\"325\\\" y=\\\"286\\\" text-anchor=\\\"middle\\\">cache</text>\\n227|              </g>\\n228|              <g class=\\\"cpu-core\\\">\\n229|                <rect x=\\\"42\\\" y=\\\"324\\\" width=\\\"352\\\" height=\\\"62\\\" rx=\\\"10\\\"></rect>\\n230|                <text class=\\\"core-text\\\" x=\\\"62\\\" y=\\\"361\\\">Core 4</text>\\n231|                <rect class=\\\"core-sub\\\" x=\\\"150\\\" y=\\\"338\\\" width=\\\"112\\\" height=\\\"34\\\" rx=\\\"6\\\"></rect>\\n232|                <text class=\\\"core-subtext\\\" x=\\\"206\\\" y=\\\"360\\\" text-anchor=\\\"middle\\\">control</text>\\n233|                <rect class=\\\"core-sub\\\" x=\\\"272\\\" y=\\\"338\\\" width=\\\"106\\\" height=\\\"34\\\" rx=\\\"6\\\"></rect>\\n234|                <text class=\\\"core-subtext\\\" x=\\\"325\\\" y=\\\"360\\\" text-anchor=\\\"middle\\\">cache</text>\\n235|              </g>\\n236|              <text class=\\\"panel-foot\\\" x=\\\"42\\\" y=\\\"404\\\">Optimised for latency: one hard job, finished fast.</text>\\n237|            </g>\\n238|\\n239|            <g class=\\\"panel\\\">\\n240|              <rect class=\\\"panel-bg\\\" x=\\\"480\\\" y=\\\"16\\\" width=\\\"404\\\" height=\\\"398\\\" rx=\\\"18\\\"></rect>\\n241|              <text class=\\\"panel-title\\\" x=\\\"506\\\" y=\\\"58\\\">GPU</text>\\n242|              <text class=\\\"panel-sub\\\" x=\\\"506\\\" y=\\\"82\\\">thousands of simple cores</text>\\n243|              <rect x=\\\"506\\\" y=\\\"102\\\" width=\\\"352\\\" height=\\\"284\\\" rx=\\\"10\\\" fill=\\\"url(#manyCorePattern)\\\" opacity=\\\"0.95\\\"></rect>\\n244|              <text class=\\\"panel-foot\\\" x=\\\"506\\\" y=\\\"404\\\">Optimised for throughput: millions of easy jobs, finished together.</text>\\n245|            </g>\\n246|          </svg>\\n247|        </div>\\n248|\\n249|        <div class=\\\"split-text\\\">\\n250|          <div class=\\\"compare-cards\\\">\\n251|            <article class=\\\"mini-card\\\">\\n252|              <h3>Latency-oriented</h3>\\n253|              <p>\\n254|                A CPU is judged by how quickly <em>one</em> thread of work finishes. Big caches keep\\n255|                data close, branch predictors guess the next move, and deep out-of-order windows\\n256|                keep a small number of instruction streams flowing. Great for a compiler, a database\\n257|                query planner, or an operating system.\\n258|              </p>\\n259|            </article>\\n260|            <article class=\\\"mini-card\\\">\\n261|              <h3>Throughput-oriented</h3>\\n262|              <p>\\n263|                A GPU gives up almost all of that. Its cores are simple ALUs with tiny register\\n264|                files. Instead of predicting branches it masks them off and runs both paths. It hides\\n265|                memory latency not with cache but with <strong>other work</strong>: while thousands of\\n266|                groups of threads wait on memory, thousands more are ready to execute.\\n267|              </p>\\n268|            </article>\\n269|          </div>\\n270|          <ul class=\\\"tick-list\\\">\\n271|            <li><strong>Same instruction, many data.</strong> Shading a 4K frame is one small program run on ~8 million pixels. Nothing waits on anything else.</li>\\n272|            <li><strong>Wide and shallow.</strong> A CPU hides latency by reducing it; a GPU hides latency by having enough threads that the wait is invisible.</li>\\n273|            <li><strong>Branching is the enemy.</strong> Divergent code paths serialise the group of threads executing together, so GPUs are at their worst on pointer-chasing, irregular, branchy code.</li>\\n274|            <li><strong>The arithmetic is shared.</strong> Because the scheduler issues one instruction to 32 or 64 lanes at a time, a GPU needs one control unit per group, not per core &mdash; that is where the transistor budget for thousands of cores comes from.</li>\\n275|          </ul>\\n276|        </div>\\n277|      </div>\\n278|    </div>\\n279|  </section>\\n280|\\n281|  <!-- =========================== INSIDE =========================== -->\\n282|  <section id=\\\"inside\\\" class=\\\"section\\\" aria-labelledby=\\\"inside-title\\\">\\n283|    <div class=\\\"wrap\\\">\\n284|      <p class=\\\"eyebrow\\\">Anatomy</p>\\n285|      <h2 id=\\\"inside-title\\\">Inside a GPU</h2>\\n286|      <p class=\\\"section-lede\\\">\\n287|        Vendors market GPUs with a wall of numbers, but almost all of them describe one of six\\n288|        things. Here is what each block actually does.\\n289|      </p>\\n290|\\n291|      <ul class=\\\"card-grid\\\">\\n292|        <li class=\\\"card reveal\\\">\\n293|          <span class=\\\"card-icon\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\">\\n294|            <svg viewBox=\\\"0 0 24 24\\\" focusable=\\\"false\\\"><rect x=\\\"3\\\" y=\\\"3\\\" width=\\\"8\\\" height=\\\"8\\\" rx=\\\"2\\\"></rect><rect x=\\\"13\\\" y=\\\"3\\\" width=\\\"8\\\" height=\\\"8\\\" rx=\\\"2\\\"></rect><rect x=\\\"3\\\" y=\\\"13\\\" width=\\\"8\\\" height=\\\"8\\\" rx=\\\"2\\\"></rect><rect x=\\\"13\\\" y=\\\"13\\\" width=\\\"8\\\" height=\\\"8\\\" rx=\\\"2\\\"></rect></svg>\\n295|          </span>\\n296|          <h3>Streaming multiprocessors (SMs)</h3>\\n297|          <p>\\n298|            The GPU's basic building block, repeated 20 to 300+ times. Each SM contains its own\\n299|            shader cores, register file, shared memory, warp schedulers and (on modern parts) tensor\\n300|            and ray-tracing units. Spec sheets quote SM counts, but the useful number is how many\\n301|            warps one SM can keep resident at a time &mdash; that is what hides memory latency.\\n302|          </p>\\n303|        </li>\\n304|\\n305|        <li class=\\\"card reveal\\\">\\n306|          <span class=\\\"card-icon\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\">\\n307|            <svg viewBox=\\\"0 0 24 24\\\" focusable=\\\"false\\\"><circle cx=\\\"7\\\" cy=\\\"7\\\" r=\\\"3.2\\\"></circle><circle cx=\\\"17\\\" cy=\\\"7\\\" r=\\\"3.2\\\"></circle><circle cx=\\\"7\\\" cy=\\\"17\\\" r=\\\"3.2\\\"></circle><circle cx=\\\"17\\\" cy=\\\"17\\\" r=\\\"3.2\\\"></circle></svg>\\n308|          </span>\\n309|          <h3>Shader cores</h3>\\n310|          <p>\\n311|            Also called CUDA cores by NVIDIA, stream processors by AMD, or Xe vector engines by\\n312|            Intel. These do the general maths: one fused multiply-add per clock per lane on most\\n313|            architectures. Shader-core count times clock speed times two is, roughly, the FP32\\n314|            throughput quoted in TFLOPS.\\n315|          </p>\\n316|        </li>\\n317|\\n318|        <li class=\\\"card reveal\\\">\\n319|          <span class=\\\"card-icon\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\">\\n320|            <svg viewBox=\\\"0 0 24 24\\\" focusable=\\\"false\\\"><rect x=\\\"2.5\\\" y=\\\"2.5\\\" width=\\\"19\\\" height=\\\"19\\\" rx=\\\"3\\\"></rect><path d=\\\"M7 7h4v4H7zM13 13h4v4h-4zM13 7h4M7 13v4\\\"></path></svg>\\n321|          </span>\\n322|          <h3>Tensor cores</h3>\\n323|          <p>\\n324|            Dedicated matrix multipliers, introduced on NVIDIA Volta in 2017 (AMD's equivalent is\\n325|            the matrix core in CDNA, Intel's is XMX). They take small tiles of numbers and compute\\n326|            <code>D = A&times;B + C</code> in one step at reduced precision. Neural networks are\\n327|            almost entirely matrix multiplies, so this is the single biggest reason deep learning\\n328|            exploded on GPUs: the same silicon delivers an order of magnitude more AI maths per watt.\\n329|          </p>\\n330|        </li>\\n331|\\n332|        <li class=\\\"card reveal\\\">\\n333|          <span class=\\\"card-icon\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\">\\n334|            <svg viewBox=\\\"0 0 24 24\\\" focusable=\\\"false\\\"><path d=\\\"M12 2.5 21 12l-9 9.5L3 12z\\\"></path><path d=\\\"M12 7.5 16.5 12 12 16.5 7.5 12z\\\"></path></svg>\\n335|          </span>\\n336|          <h3>Ray-tracing cores</h3>\\n337|          <p>\\n338|            Added on NVIDIA Turing (2018) as RT cores, and on AMD RDNA 2 as ray accelerators.\\n339|            They accelerate exactly three things: testing a ray against a bounding-volume\\n340|            hierarchy, testing it against triangles, and shading the hit. What used to be a\\n341|            software traversal loop becomes a hardware instruction, which is why real-time ray\\n342|            tracing became practical without melting the shaders.\\n343|          </p>\\n344|        </li>\\n345|\\n346|        <li class=\\\"card reveal\\\">\\n347|          <span class=\\\"card-icon\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\">\\n348|            <svg viewBox=\\\"0 0 24 24\\\" focusable=\\\"false\\\"><rect x=\\\"3\\\" y=\\\"6\\\" width=\\\"18\\\" height=\\\"12\\\" rx=\\\"2.5\\\"></rect><path d=\\\"M7 10h10M7 14h6\\\"></path></svg>\\n349|          </span>\\n350|          <h3>VRAM and its type</h3>\\n351|          <p>\\n352|            VRAM holds whatever the GPU is working on right now: textures, framebuffers, model\\n353|            weights, KV cache. Capacity decides whether a job <em>fits</em>. Consumer cards use\\n354|            GDDR6 or GDDR7 over a 128&ndash;512-bit bus; datacenter parts use HBM3/HBM3e, which\\n355|            stacks memory dies next to the logic on an interposer for bus widths of 4096&ndash;8192 bits.\\n356|            Running out of VRAM means spilling to system memory over PCIe, which is 10&ndash;60&times; slower.\\n357|          </p>\\n358|        </li>\\n359|\\n360|        <li class=\\\"card reveal\\\">\\n361|          <span class=\\\"card-icon\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\">\\n362|            <svg viewBox=\\\"0 0 24 24\\\" focusable=\\\"false\\\"><path d=\\\"M2.5 12h19M6 7v10M10 7v10M14 7v10M18 7v10\\\"></path></svg>\\n363|          </span>\\n364|          <h3>Memory bus and bandwidth</h3>\\n365|          <p>\\n366|            The bus is the number of wires between GPU and memory; bandwidth is bus width &times;\\n367|            memory clock &times; transfers per clock. Bandwidth, not TFLOPS, is usually the real\\n368|            limit: an H100 SXM5 moves 3.35 TB/s while a GeForce RTX 4090 moves 1.0 TB/s, and HBM\\n369|            parts go far beyond that. Feed the shaders faster and the same core count gets faster;\\n370|            this is why a memory overclock so often beats a core overclock.\\n371|          </p>\\n372|        </li>\\n373|      </ul>\\n374|    </div>\\n375|  </section>\\n376|\\n377|  <!-- ========================== COMPARE =========================== -->\\n378|  <section id=\\\"compare\\\" class=\\\"section\\\" aria-labelledby=\\\"compare-title\\\">\\n379|    <div class=\\\"wrap\\\">\\n380|      <p class=\\\"eyebrow\\\">The data</p>\\n381|      <h2 id=\\\"compare-title\\\">Compare 15 GPUs</h2>\\n382|      <p class=\\\"section-lede\\\">\\n383|        Fifteen parts spanning 2020 to 2025, from a $249 entry GPU to a 192&nbsp;GB accelerator.\\n384|        Search by name, filter by segment, and click any column header to sort. Click a row to\\n385|        expand its notes and the vendor pages the numbers came from.\\n386|      </p>\\n387|\\n388|      <div class=\\\"toolbar\\\">\\n389|        <div class=\\\"field\\\">\\n390|          <label for=\\\"search\\\">Search by name, vendor or architecture</label>\\n391|          <input type=\\\"search\\\" id=\\\"search\\\" name=\\\"search\\\" placeholder=\\\"4090, H100, Radeon&hellip;\\\" autocomplete=\\\"off\\\" spellcheck=\\\"false\\\">\\n392|        </div>\\n393|        <div class=\\\"field\\\">\\n394|          <span class=\\\"field-label\\\" id=\\\"segment-label\\\">Segment</span>\\n395|          <div class=\\\"chips\\\" role=\\\"group\\\" aria-labelledby=\\\"segment-label\\\">\\n396|            <button type=\\\"button\\\" class=\\\"chip\\\" data-segment=\\\"All\\\" aria-pressed=\\\"true\\\">All</button>\\n397|            <button type=\\\"button\\\" class=\\\"chip\\\" data-segment=\\\"Consumer\\\" aria-pressed=\\\"false\\\">Consumer</button>\\n398|            <button type=\\\"button\\\" class=\\\"chip\\\" data-segment=\\\"Datacenter\\\" aria-pressed=\\\"false\\\">Datacenter</button>\\n399|          </div>\\n400|        </div>\\n401|        <button type=\\\"button\\\" class=\\\"btn btn-ghost btn-small\\\" id=\\\"reset-btn\\\">Reset</button>\\n402|      </div>\\n403|\\n404|      <p class=\\\"result-count\\\" id=\\\"result-count\\\" role=\\\"status\\\" aria-live=\\\"polite\\\">Showing 15 of 15 GPUs</p>\\n405|\\n406|      <div class=\\\"table-shell\\\">\\n407|        <div class=\\\"table-scroll\\\" id=\\\"table-scroll\\\" role=\\\"region\\\" tabindex=\\\"0\\\" aria-labelledby=\\\"compare-title\\\">\\n408|          <table id=\\\"gpu-table\\\">\\n409|            <caption>\\n410|              Vendor-published specifications for 15 GPUs. A dash (&mdash;) means the vendor does\\n411|              not publish that figure. AI TOPS figures are vendor marketing numbers measured at\\n412|              mixed low precision and are not comparable across vendors.\\n413|            </caption>\\n414|            <thead>\\n415|              <tr>\\n416|                <th scope=\\\"col\\\" data-key=\\\"name\\\" class=\\\"col-sticky\\\"><button type=\\\"button\\\" class=\\\"sort\\\" data-sort=\\\"name\\\">GPU<span class=\\\"sort-ind\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\"></span></button></th>\\n417|                <th scope=\\\"col\\\" data-key=\\\"vendor\\\"><button type=\\\"button\\\" class=\\\"sort\\\" data-sort=\\\"vendor\\\">Vendor<span class=\\\"sort-ind\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\"></span></button></th>\\n418|                <th scope=\\\"col\\\" data-key=\\\"segment\\\"><button type=\\\"button\\\" class=\\\"sort\\\" data-sort=\\\"segment\\\">Segment<span class=\\\"sort-ind\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\"></span></button></th>\\n419|                <th scope=\\\"col\\\" data-key=\\\"arch\\\"><span class=\\\"static-head\\\">Architecture</span></th>\\n420|                <th scope=\\\"col\\\" data-key=\\\"year\\\" class=\\\"num\\\"><button type=\\\"button\\\" class=\\\"sort\\\" data-sort=\\\"year\\\">Year<span class=\\\"sort-ind\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\"></span></button></th>\\n421|                <th scope=\\\"col\\\" data-key=\\\"node\\\"><span class=\\\"static-head\\\">Process</span></th>\\n422|                <th scope=\\\"col\\\" data-key=\\\"vramGB\\\" class=\\\"num\\\"><button type=\\\"button\\\" class=\\\"sort\\\" data-sort=\\\"vramGB\\\">VRAM (GB)<span class=\\\"sort-ind\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\"></span></button></th>\\n423|                <th scope=\\\"col\\\" data-key=\\\"memType\\\"><span class=\\\"static-head\\\">Memory</span></th>\\n424|                <th scope=\\\"col\\\" data-key=\\\"busBits\\\" class=\\\"num\\\"><button type=\\\"button\\\" class=\\\"sort\\\" data-sort=\\\"busBits\\\">Bus (bit)<span class=\\\"sort-ind\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\"></span></button></th>\\n425|                <th scope=\\\"col\\\" data-key=\\\"bandwidthGBs\\\" class=\\\"num\\\"><button type=\\\"button\\\" class=\\\"sort\\\" data-sort=\\\"bandwidthGBs\\\">Bandwidth (GB/s)<span class=\\\"sort-ind\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\"></span></button></th>\\n426|                <th scope=\\\"col\\\" data-key=\\\"tdpW\\\" class=\\\"num\\\"><button type=\\\"button\\\" class=\\\"sort\\\" data-sort=\\\"tdpW\\\">TDP (W)<span class=\\\"sort-ind\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\"></span></button></th>\\n427|                <th scope=\\\"col\\\" data-key=\\\"msrpUSD\\\" class=\\\"num\\\"><button type=\\\"button\\\" class=\\\"sort\\\" data-sort=\\\"msrpUSD\\\">MSRP (USD)<span class=\\\"sort-ind\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\"></span></button></th>\\n428|                <th scope=\\\"col\\\" data-key=\\\"fp32\\\" class=\\\"num\\\"><button type=\\\"button\\\" class=\\\"sort\\\" data-sort=\\\"fp32\\\">FP32 (TFLOPS)<span class=\\\"sort-ind\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\"></span></button></th>\\n429|                <th scope=\\\"col\\\" data-key=\\\"aiTops\\\" class=\\\"num\\\"><button type=\\\"button\\\" class=\\\"sort\\\" data-sort=\\\"aiTops\\\">AI TOPS<span class=\\\"sort-ind\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\"></span></button></th>\\n430|              </tr>\\n431|            </thead>\\n432|            <tbody id=\\\"gpu-tbody\\\">\\n433|              <!-- Rows are rendered by script.js from the GPUS array. -->\\n434|            </tbody>\\n435|          </table>\\n436|        </div>\\n437|        <p class=\\\"scroll-hint\\\" id=\\\"scroll-hint\\\" hidden>\\n438|          <span aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\">&larr;&rarr;</span> Scroll the table sideways to see the remaining columns.\\n439|        </p>\\n440|      </div>\\n441|\\n442|      <noscript>\\n443|        <p class=\\\"callout\\\">\\n444|          This table is built from a data array by JavaScript, so it needs scripting enabled to\\n445|          render. The numbers themselves come from the vendor pages listed in the README.\\n446|        </p>\\n447|      </noscript>\\n448|    </div>\\n449|  </section>\\n450|\\n451|  <!-- ========================= USE CASES ========================== -->\\n452|  <section id=\\\"use-cases\\\" class=\\\"section\\\" aria-labelledby=\\\"usecases-title\\\">\\n453|    <div class=\\\"wrap\\\">\\n454|      <p class=\\\"eyebrow\\\">What people run on them</p>\\n455|      <h2 id=\\\"usecases-title\\\">Use cases: what actually matters</h2>\\n456|      <p class=\\\"section-lede\\\">\\n457|        Every workload stresses a different part of the chip. If you know which number matters,\\n458|        you can read a spec sheet in about five seconds.\\n459|      </p>\\n460|\\n461|      <ul class=\\\"case-grid\\\">\\n462|        <li class=\\\"case-card reveal\\\">\\n463|          <h3>Gaming and real-time rendering</h3>\\n464|          <p class=\\\"case-matters\\\"><span class=\\\"tag\\\">What matters</span> shader throughput, RT cores, VRAM capacity, upscaling quality</p>\\n465|          <p>\\n466|            A frame must be drawn in about 8&nbsp;ms, so latency and frame-time consistency matter as\\n467|            much as average speed. Rendering is a straightforward parallel workload, which is why\\n468|            GPUs were invented for it. At 4K with ray tracing on, the RT cores and the memory bus\\n469|            carry the load, and 12&nbsp;GB of VRAM starts to bite in the newest titles. Frame\\n470|            generation and DLSS/FSR do not add hardware performance &mdash; they change what has to\\n471|            be computed.\\n472|          </p>\\n473|        </li>\\n474|\\n475|        <li class=\\\"case-card reveal\\\">\\n476|          <h3>AI training</h3>\\n477|          <p class=\\\"case-matters\\\"><span class=\\\"tag\\\">What matters</span> tensor-core maths at low precision, VRAM capacity, interconnect bandwidth</p>\\n478|          <p>\\n479|            Training is one gigantic, weeks-long burst of matrix multiplies. Two numbers dominate:\\n480|            how fast the tensor cores chew through BF16/FP8/FP4 tiles, and whether the whole\\n481|            model, its gradients and its optimiser state fit in VRAM (rule of thumb: 16&ndash;20\\n482|            bytes of memory per parameter at mixed precision). When a job spans many GPUs, the\\n483|            interconnect &mdash; NVLink at 900&nbsp;GB/s per direction on Hopper, or PCIe at\\n484|            ~64&nbsp;GB/s &mdash; decides how much of that compute is wasted waiting for gradients.\\n485|          </p>\\n486|        </li>\\n487|\\n488|        <li class=\\\"case-card reveal\\\">\\n489|          <h3>AI inference</h3>\\n490|          <p class=\\\"case-matters\\\"><span class=\\\"tag\\\">What matters</span> memory bandwidth per dollar, VRAM capacity, batch size</p>\\n491|          <p>\\n492|            Generating one token is not compute-bound, it is memory-bound: every weight has to be\\n493|            read from VRAM to produce a single number, then the next token starts over. So tokens\\n494|            per second scale with <em>bandwidth</em>, not TFLOPS &mdash; a fact that surprises\\n495|            people who buy the fastest compute card available. This is why quantisation (INT8,\\n496|            INT4) helps so much: smaller weights mean less data to move per token, and why\\n497|            consumer cards with 24&nbsp;GB or more punch above their weight for local models.\\n498|          </p>\\n499|        </li>\\n500|\\n501|        <li class=\\\"case-card reveal\\\">\\n502|          <h3>Scientific simulation</h3>\\n503|          <p class=\\\"case-matters\\\"><span class=\\\"tag\\\">What matters</span> FP64 throughput on some codes, ECC memory, capacity</p>\\n504|          <p>\\n505|            Molecular dynamics, CFD, weather and lattice QCD map cleanly onto GPU parallelism, and\\n506|            many codes need double precision the consumer parts barely have (datacenter GPUs\\n507|            dedicate real silicon to FP64; GeForce and Radeon cards emulate it). ECC memory matters\\n508|            here because a silently corrupted bit ruins a week-long run, and some projects need\\n509|            capacity that only HBM accelerators or unified memory can provide.\\n510|          </p>\\n511|        </li>\\n512|\\n513|        <li class=\\\"case-card reveal\\\">\\n514|          <h3>Content creation and rendering</h3>\\n515|          <p class=\\\"case-matters\\\"><span class=\\\"tag\\\">What matters</span> VRAM headroom, hardware video encode/decode, driver stability</p>\\n516|          <p>\\n517|            Offline renderers (OptiX, Redshift, Cycles, Blender) are pure throughput workloads and\\n518|            scale nearly linearly with cores. Video work leans on dedicated encode/decode blocks\\n519|            rather than shaders, so codec support beats raw TFLOPS. The practical constraint for\\n520|            8K timelines and big scene files is VRAM: when it runs out, software falls back to\\n521|            system RAM and the render slows to a crawl.\\n522|          </p>\\n523|        </li>\\n524|\\n525|        <li class=\\\"case-card case-card--dead reveal\\\">\\n526|          <h3>Cryptocurrency mining &mdash; now a footnote</h3>\\n527|          <p class=\\\"case-matters\\\"><span class=\\\"tag\\\">What matters</span> nothing that a modern GPU buyer should care about</p>\\n528|          <p>\\n529|            GPU mining mattered because proof-of-work is a hash loop that parallelises perfectly.\\n530|            Two things killed it. First, ASICs &mdash; purpose-built chips do SHA-256 thousands of\\n531|            times more efficiently than any GPU, so bitcoin mining left GPUs behind years ago.\\n532|            Second, Ethereum's move to proof-of-stake in September 2022 removed the largest\\n533|            remaining GPU-mined network overnight, dumping a huge second-hand supply onto the\\n534|            market. Today GPUs are bought for graphics, simulation and AI. If someone is selling\\n535|            you a card for mining returns, that is the scam, not the business model.\\n536|          </p>\\n537|        </li>\\n538|      </ul>\\n539|    </div>\\n540|  </section>\\n541|\\n542|  <!-- ========================== TIMELINE ========================== -->\\n543|  <section id=\\\"timeline\\\" class=\\\"section\\\" aria-labelledby=\\\"timeline-title\\\">\\n544|    <div class=\\\"wrap\\\">\\n545|      <p class=\\\"eyebrow\\\">Twenty years in twelve steps</p>\\n546|      <h2 id=\\\"timeline-title\\\">GPU generation timeline, 2006 &rarr; 2025</h2>\\n547|      <p class=\\\"section-lede\\\">\\n548|        Two long arcs: first the unification of shading hardware, then the addition of specialised\\n549|        units for AI and ray tracing.\\n550|      </p>\\n551|\\n552|      <ol class=\\\"timeline\\\">\\n553|        <li class=\\\"tl-item reveal\\\">\\n554|          <span class=\\\"tl-year\\\">2006</span>\\n555|          <div class=\\\"tl-body\\\">\\n556|            <h3>G80 &mdash; GeForce 8800 GTX <span class=\\\"tl-tag\\\">NVIDIA</span></h3>\\n557|            <p>The dividing line. G80 replaced separate vertex and pixel pipelines with 128 unified shaders that could run either job, introduced CUDA as a general-purpose compute platform, and made the GPU programmable in C. Everything after this is an argument about how many cores and how wide a bus.</p>\\n558|          </div>\\n559|        </li>\\n560|        <li class=\\\"tl-item reveal\\\">\\n561|          <span class=\\\"tl-year\\\">2010</span>\\n562|          <div class=\\\"tl-body\\\">\\n563|            <h3>Fermi &mdash; GF100 <span class=\\\"tl-tag\\\">NVIDIA</span></h3>\\n564|            <p>Added a proper cache hierarchy, ECC memory on the compute parts, and real double-precision units. Fermi made GPUs credible for scientific computing rather than just fast graphics.</p>\\n565|          </div>\\n566|        </li>\\n567|        <li class=\\\"tl-item reveal\\\">\\n568|          <span class=\\\"tl-year\\\">2012</span>\\n569|          <div class=\\\"tl-body\\\">\\n570|            <h3>Kepler &mdash; GK104 / GK110 <span class=\\\"tl-tag\\\">NVIDIA</span></h3>\\n571|            <p>Efficiency over brute force: many simpler cores, dynamic parallelism, and the GK110 die powering the first Titan and the Tesla K20. GPUs start showing up in supercomputer announcements.</p>\\n572|          </div>\\n573|        </li>\\n574|        <li class=\\\"tl-item reveal\\\">\\n575|          <span class=\\\"tl-year\\\">2014</span>\\n576|          <div class=\\\"tl-body\\\">\\n577|            <h3>Maxwell &mdash; GM204 <span class=\\\"tl-tag\\\">NVIDIA</span></h3>\\n578|            <p>A large jump in performance per watt, mostly from re-partitioning the SM and adding better compression for colour and memory traffic. The GTX 970/980 era made high-end PC gaming mainstream.</p>\\n579|          </div>\\n580|        </li>\\n581|        <li class=\\\"tl-item reveal\\\">\\n582|          <span class=\\\"tl-year\\\">2016</span>\\n583|          <div class=\\\"tl-body\\\">\\n584|            <h3>Pascal &mdash; GP100 / GP104 <span class=\\\"tl-tag\\\">NVIDIA</span></h3>\\n585|            <p>HBM2 on the datacenter part, GDDR5X on consumer, and the P100 pair used to train early large neural networks. AMD's Polaris ships the same year, and the modern compute stack (cuDNN, cuBLAS) matures here.</p>\\n586|          </div>\\n587|        </li>\\n588|        <li class=\\\"tl-item reveal\\\">\\n589|          <span class=\\\"tl-year\\\">2017</span>\\n590|          <div class=\\\"tl-body\\\">\\n591|            <h3>Volta &mdash; GV100 <span class=\\\"tl-tag tl-tag--key\\\">tensor cores</span></h3>\\n592|            <p>The big pivot. Volta's tensor cores multiply small matrices in one instruction, and NVLink plus HBM2 feed them fast enough to matter. Training times for neural networks fall by roughly 10&times; versus Pascal in a single generation.</p>\\n593|          </div>\\n594|        </li>\\n595|        <li class=\\\"tl-item reveal\\\">\\n596|          <span class=\\\"tl-year\\\">2018</span>\\n597|          <div class=\\\"tl-body\\\">\\n598|            <h3>Turing &mdash; TU102 <span class=\\\"tl-tag tl-tag--key\\\">RT cores</span></h3>\\n599|            <p>Ray-tracing cores arrive, along with shaders able to run integer and float work at the same time and deep-learning super-sampling. Real-time ray tracing stops being a demo and becomes a product, even on the first generation of games that only partly used it.</p>\\n600|          </div>\\n601|        </li>\\n602|        <li class=\\\"tl-item reveal\\\">\\n603|          <span class=\\\"tl-year\\\">2020</span>\\n604|          <div class=\\\"tl-body\\\">\\n605|            <h3>Ampere &mdash; GA102 / A100 <span class=\\\"tl-tag\\\">NVIDIA</span></h3>\\n606|            <p>A100 pairs 3rd-generation tensor cores with sparse matrix support and 2039&nbsp;GB/s of HBM2e, becoming the default accelerator for the first large transformer era. Ampere consumer cards become the mining-era poster children. AMD releases RDNA 2 with ray accelerators and unified memory access the same autumn.</p>\\n607|          </div>\\n608|        </li>\\n609|        <li class=\\\"tl-item reveal\\\">\\n610|          <span class=\\\"tl-year\\\">2022</span>\\n611|          <div class=\\\"tl-body\\\">\\n612|            <h3>Hopper &mdash; H100 <span class=\\\"tl-tag tl-tag--key\\\">transformers in silicon</span></h3>\\n613|            <p>Built around the transformer: a dedicated Transformer Engine that negotiates FP8 precision per layer, thread-block clusters for communicating SMs, and NVLink at 900&nbsp;GB/s. H100 is the part most of the 2022&ndash;2024 AI boom is measured in.</p>\\n614|          </div>\\n615|        </li>\\n616|        <li class=\\\"tl-item reveal\\\">\\n617|          <span class=\\\"tl-year\\\">2022</span>\\n618|          <div class=\\\"tl-body\\\">\\n619|            <h3>Ada Lovelace &mdash; AD102 <span class=\\\"tl-tag\\\">NVIDIA</span></h3>\\n620|            <p>The consumer counterpart: 4th-generation tensor cores, 3rd-generation RT cores, and optical flow hardware for frame generation. The RTX 4090 sets a consumer bar that stays at the top for two years.</p>\\n621|          </div>\\n622|        </li>\\n623|        <li class=\\\"tl-item reveal\\\">\\n624|          <span class=\\\"tl-year\\\">2024</span>\\n625|          <div class=\\\"tl-body\\\">\\n626|            <h3>Blackwell &mdash; B200 / GB200 <span class=\\\"tl-tag tl-tag--key\\\">two dies, one GPU</span></h3>\\n627|            <p>A datacenter GPU assembled from two reticle-sized dies joined by a 10&nbsp;TB/s die-to-die link, with 192&nbsp;GB of HBM3e and FP4 tensor maths. This is the generation where the accelerator, not the CPU, became the most expensive component in a datacenter.</p>\\n628|          </div>\\n629|        </li>\\n630|        <li class=\\\"tl-item reveal\\\">\\n631|          <span class=\\\"tl-year\\\">2025</span>\\n632|          <div class=\\\"tl-body\\\">\\n633|            <h3>Blackwell for consumers &mdash; RTX 50 series <span class=\\\"tl-tag\\\">NVIDIA</span></h3>\\n634|            <p>GDDR7 across the stack, 32&nbsp;GB on the flagship, and neural rendering pushed further. On the other side of the market, AMD's RDNA 4 and Intel's Battlemage compete on price per frame rather than on absolute performance &mdash; a healthy thing for an industry that had drifted upward.</p>\\n635|          </div>\\n636|        </li>\\n637|      </ol>\\n638|    </div>\\n639|  </section>\\n640|\\n641|  <!-- ============================= FAQ ============================ -->\\n642|  <section id=\\\"faq\\\" class=\\\"section\\\" aria-labelledby=\\\"faq-title\\\">\\n643|    <div class=\\\"wrap wrap-narrow\\\">\\n644|      <p class=\\\"eyebrow\\\">Questions people actually ask</p>\\n645|      <h2 id=\\\"faq-title\\\">FAQ</h2>\\n646|\\n647|      <div class=\\\"faq\\\">\\n648|        <details>\\n649|          <summary>Do I need a GPU for AI?</summary>\\n650|          <p>\\n651|            Not strictly &mdash; you can run a small model on a CPU, and the smallest quantised\\n652|            models run acceptably on a modern laptop chip. But the CPU has a fraction of the memory\\n653|            bandwidth and almost none of the matrix-multiply hardware, so a model that takes a\\n654|            second per token on a CPU can run many times faster on a mid-range GPU. The honest\\n655|            answer is: for learning and small local experiments, a GPU is convenient; for training\\n656|            anything serious, it is not optional, because the CPU equivalent would take years.\\n657|          </p>\\n658|        </details>\\n659|\\n660|        <details>\\n661|          <summary>What is VRAM actually for?</summary>\\n662|          <p>\\n663|            It is the GPU's working set, and it comes in two distinct flavours. For graphics it holds\\n664|            textures, geometry buffers, render targets and compiled shaders. For AI it holds model\\n665|            weights, activations during the forward pass, gradients and optimiser state during\\n666|            training, and the KV cache during inference &mdash; which grows with every token of\\n667|            context you keep. Capacity decides whether a job fits at all; once you exceed it, the\\n668|            driver shuffles data over PCIe and throughput collapses. Speed (bandwidth) decides how\\n669|            fast the job runs once it does fit.\\n670|          </p>\\n671|        </details>\\n672|\\n673|        <details>\\n674|          <summary>Is more TFLOPS always faster?</summary>\\n675|          <p>\\n676|            No, and the gap can be large. FP32 TFLOPS is a peak number that assumes perfectly\\n677|            coalesced memory access, no branching, and a core that never waits. Real workloads wait\\n678|            on memory constantly, so bandwidth, cache behaviour and occupancy decide the outcome.\\n679|            Precision matters too: a card quoting 1000 TFLOPS at FP4 cannot run a workload that\\n680|            needs FP16 or FP64 at anything like that rate, and FP32 figures published by different\\n681|            vendors are measured differently (base versus boost clock). Read TFLOPS as a ceiling,\\n682|            never a prediction.\\n683|          </p>\\n684|        </details>\\n685|\\n686|        <details>\\n687|          <summary>Why are datacenter GPUs so expensive?</summary>\\n688|          <p>\\n689|            Three reasons. They use HBM3e, which is far more costly per gigabyte than GDDR because\\n690|            the memory dies are stacked and bonded onto a silicon interposer next to the GPU die.\\n691|            They are enormous &mdash; at reticle limits, so yield per wafer is poor. And their\\n692|            supply is constrained by advanced packaging capacity, not just by wafer starts. Add\\n693|            software and support contracts for a market that will not tolerate unplanned downtime,\\n694|            and you get prices that look absurd next to a gaming card with similar raw TFLOPS.\\n695|            Vendor list prices for datacenter parts are usually not published at all, which is why\\n696|            the table shows a dash for them.\\n697|          </p>\\n698|        </details>\\n699|\\n700|        <details>\\n701|          <summary>What is CUDA?</summary>\\n702|          <p>\\n703|            NVIDIA's parallel computing platform, released with the G80 architecture in 2006:\\n704|            a C/C++ dialect with extensions for kernel functions that run across thousands of\\n705|            threads, plus the compiler that maps them onto the hardware. It has since grown into a\\n706|            fortress &mdash; cuBLAS, cuDNN, NCCL, TensorRT, and every major framework targeting it\\n707|            first. Its practical importance is not the language but the ecosystem: the reason an AI\\n708|            researcher reaches for NVIDIA hardware is that so little code needs rewriting to use it.\\n709|            Open alternatives exist (OpenCL, SYCL, and AMD's HIP, which is close enough to CUDA to\\n710|            port mechanically), but they trail in library coverage.\\n711|          </p>\\n712|        </details>\\n713|\\n714|        <details>\\n715|          <summary>What is the difference between consumer and datacenter GPUs?</summary>\\n716|          <p>\\n717|            The same architecture, tuned for different constraints. Consumer cards optimise cost\\n718|            per frame at a 300&ndash;600&nbsp;W board power, with limited VRAM over a narrow bus,\\n719|            no ECC, and thermals designed for a case. Datacenter parts optimise sustained work per\\n720|            watt and per rack unit: HBM on a huge bus, ECC everywhere, higher FP64, NVLink or\\n721|            Infinity Fabric for scaling out, and a form factor that expects datacentre airflow.\\n722|            A workstation-class consumer card can beat a datacenter card on a single-frame render\\n723|            and lose badly on a 40-hour training run, because the datacenter card never throttles\\n724|            and never runs out of memory.\\n725|          </p>\\n726|        </details>\\n727|\\n728|        <details>\\n729|          <summary>Do I need tensor cores or RT cores for gaming?</summary>\\n730|          <p>\\n731|            Ray-tracing cores only help in games that use them, which is now most new AAA releases\\n732|            but still a minority of what is installed. Tensor cores matter indirectly: they run\\n733|            DLSS and frame generation, which are increasingly how performance tiers are reached at\\n734|            4K. Neither is necessary for rasterised e-sports titles at high frame rates, where fast\\n735|            shaders and a decent memory bus do all the work. Buying for the features your actual\\n736|            games support is more sensible than buying for the peak number on the box.\\n737|          </p>\\n738|        </details>\\n739|\\n740|        <details>\\n741|          <summary>How much VRAM do I need?</summary>\\n742|          <p>\\n743|            For 1080p gaming, 8&nbsp;GB is workable today and 12&nbsp;GB is comfortable. For 1440p,\\n744|            start at 12&nbsp;GB; for 4K with ray tracing and high-resolution textures, 16&nbsp;GB or\\n745|            more is where you want to be, and some 2024&ndash;2025 titles exceed 12&nbsp;GB at\\n746|            maximum settings. For AI work the rule is arithmetic rather than taste: a 7-billion\\n747|            parameter model needs roughly 4&ndash;6&nbsp;GB quantised to 4-bit; a 70-billion\\n748|            parameter model needs 35&ndash;40&nbsp;GB even heavily quantised, which is why 24&nbsp;GB\\n749|            consumer cards and 80&ndash;192&nbsp;GB accelerators are a different category entirely.\\n750|          </p>\\n751|        </details>\\n752|      </div>\\n753|    </div>\\n754|  </section>\\n755|</main>\\n756|\\n757|<footer class=\\\"site-footer\\\">\\n758|  <div class=\\\"wrap footer-grid\\\">\\n759|    <div>\\n760|      <p class=\\\"footer-title\\\">GPU<span class=\\\"brand-accent\\\">Atlas</span></p>\\n761|      <p class=\\\"footer-note\\\">\\n762|        <strong>Data sources.</strong> All specifications in the comparison table are\\n763|        vendor-published figures taken from NVIDIA, AMD and Intel product pages and datasheets\\n764|        (linked per row under &ldquo;Sources&rdquo;). Where a vendor does not publish a figure &mdash;\\n765|        most commonly list prices and AI-throughput ratings for datacenter accelerators &mdash; the\\n766|        cell shows a dash and the caveat is recorded in that row's notes. No figure on this page is\\n767|        estimated, interpolated or inferred. Memory bandwidth is quoted as the vendor states it\\n768|        (bus width &times; effective data rate); TDP is the vendor's board power rating, not a\\n769|        measured figure.\\n770|      </p>\\n771|    </div>\\n772|    <div>\\n773|      <p class=\\\"footer-note\\\">\\n774|        Everything here is static: HTML, one CSS file and one JavaScript file, no build step, no\\n775|        network requests, no fonts or images loaded from anywhere. Open <code>index.html</code>\\n776|        directly from disk and it works offline.\\n777|      </p>\\n778|      <p class=\\\"footer-meta\\\">\\n779|        Page built <time id=\\\"build-date\\\" datetime=\\\"\\\">&hellip;</time> &middot;\\n780|        <a href=\\\"#top\\\">Back to top</a>\\n781|      </p>\\n782|    </div>\\n783|  </div>\\n784|</footer>\\n785|\\n786|<script src=\\\"script.js\\\"></script>\\n787|</body>\\n788|</html>\", \"total_lines\": 788, \"file_size\": 46791, \"truncated\": false, \"is_binary\": false, \"is_image\": false, \"not_found\": false}", "tool_call_id": "call-8f551bb3-d7d9-48a0-9848-46e51f42c23a", "tool_calls": null, "tool_name": "read_file", "effect_disposition": null, "timestamp": 1790791187.9988246, "token_count": null, "finish_reason": null, "reasoning": null, "reasoning_content": null, "reasoning_details": null, "codex_reasoning_ite