ssion_id": "20260930_225432_76ed87", "role": "tool", "content": "{\"content\": \"100|    shaderCores: 16384,\\n101|    tensorCores: 512,\\n102|    rtCores: 128,\\n103|    notes: 'Still the most useful consumer card for local AI work: 24 GB of VRAM plus 1008 GB/s of bandwidth fits quantised 30B-class models that a 16 GB card cannot. Its 1321 AI TOPS figure is at FP8 with sparsity; the FP32 number is the boost-clock rating.',\\n104|    sources: [\\n105|      'https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/geforce/graphics-cards/40-series/rtx-4090/',\\n106|      'https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/geforce/graphics-cards/40-series/'\\n107|    ]\\n108|  },\\n109|  {\\n110|    name: 'GeForce RTX 4080 SUPER',\\n111|    vendor: 'NVIDIA',\\n112|    segment: 'Consumer',\\n113|    arch: 'Ada Lovelace',\\n114|    year: 2024,\\n115|    node: 'TSMC 4N',\\n116|    vramGB: 16,\\n117|    memType: 'GDDR6X',\\n118|    busBits: 256,\\n119|    bandwidthGBs: 736,\\n120|    tdpW: 320,\\n121|    msrpUSD: 999,\\n122|    fp32: 52.2,\\n123|    aiTops: 836,\\n124|    shaderCores: 10240,\\n125|    tensorCores: 320,\\n126|    rtCores: 80,\\n127|    notes: 'A mid-cycle refresh that raised clocks and memory speed over the original 4080. Included here as the clearest illustration of how much bandwidth matters: it has 25% fewer shader cores than the 4090 but 27% less bandwidth, and lands almost exactly 27% slower in practice.',\\n128|    sources: [\\n129|      'https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/geforce/graphics-cards/40-series/rtx-4080-super/'\\n130|    ]\\n131|  },\\n132|  {\\n133|    name: 'GeForce RTX 3090',\\n134|    vendor: 'NVIDIA',\\n135|    segment: 'Consumer',\\n136|    arch: 'Ampere',\\n137|    year: 2020,\\n138|    node: 'Samsung 8N',\\n139|    vramGB: 24,\\n140|    memType: 'GDDR6X',\\n141|    busBits: 384,\\n142|    bandwidthGBs: 936,\\n143|    tdpW: 350,\\n144|    msrpUSD: 1499,\\n145|    fp32: 35.6,\\n146|    aiTops: null,\\n147|    shaderCores: 10496,\\n148|    tensorCores: 328,\\n149|    rtCores: 82,\\n150|    